/* Déclic — Anglais B2 (unités 7 à 9 : Reporting verbs, Making a case, Word power). Format : docs/ANGLAIS.md */
(()=>{
// V('a ',['b','c'],'.') → ['a b.','a c.'] : toutes les combinaisons de variantes d’une réponse écrite
const V=(...parts)=>parts.reduce((acc,p)=>acc.flatMap(a=>[].concat(p).map(x=>a+x)),['']);

COURS.push(
/* ===================================================================== */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-reporting', o:7, h:'Reporting verbs', p:'Report what people said without quoting them word for word: <i>admit, deny, suggest, warn, accuse</i> and their constructions, then summarise a whole conversation in a few precise sentences.', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'A meeting that went wrong',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you’ll be able to report what people said accurately and concisely, choosing verbs such as <i>admit, deny, suggest</i> or <i>warn</i> with the right construction, and to summarise a whole conversation in a few sentences.</p>
<p>Claire runs a small design agency. On Monday morning she talks to two members of her team, Tom and Priya. <b>Listen without reading</b> first, twice if you like. The script appears after your answer.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the main problem Claire raises?</p>`,
  audio:[['Claire','Right, before we start: the client rang first thing. They still haven’t received the final designs.'],['Tom','Ah. That’s my fault, I’m afraid. I was supposed to send them on Friday and it completely slipped my mind.'],['Claire','Okay. Look, these things happen, but it can’t happen again. If we miss another deadline with them, we could lose the account.'],['Priya','To be fair to Tom, he was covering for me most of last week. Why don’t we set up a shared calendar for deadlines, so it doesn’t all depend on one person’s memory?'],['Claire','That’s a good idea. And Tom, if I were you, I’d ring them this morning and apologise in person.'],['Tom','I will. But just so you know, I didn’t ignore their emails, whatever they’re saying. I answered every single one.'],['Claire','I believe you. Let’s just get it sorted by tonight. Priya, would you mind checking the files before they go out?'],['Priya','No problem. I’ll do it straight after lunch.']], cache:true,
  opts:['The client has threatened to cancel the contract.','The client hasn’t received the designs that were due on Friday.','Tom has been ignoring the client’s emails for weeks.','Priya was off sick and missed an important deadline.'], ans:1,
  why:'The client <i>still hasn’t received the final designs</i>, which Tom was supposed to send on Friday. The client hasn’t threatened anything: it is Claire who warns that they <i>could</i> lose the account. Tom insists that he answered every email, and Priya only says Tom was covering for her.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Verbs for reporting',
  p:`<p>These verbs let you report <b>what kind of thing</b> someone said (an admission, a denial, a warning…), not just the words. Learn each one <b>with its construction</b>, shown in brackets: that is where the difficulty lies. Tap a line to hear it. In the vocabulary review, the verb alone will be enough.</p>`,
  mots:[['to admit (doing, that…)','admettre, reconnaître (avoir fait)','Tom admitted forgetting to send the designs.'],['to deny (doing, that…)','nier (avoir fait)','He denied ignoring their emails.'],['to suggest (doing, that…)','suggérer, proposer','Priya suggested setting up a shared calendar.'],['to recommend (doing, that…)','recommander, conseiller','I’d recommend booking well in advance.'],['to warn (sb not to do)','avertir, mettre en garde (qn de ne pas faire)','She warned us not to miss another deadline.'],['to accuse (sb of doing)','accuser (qn d’avoir fait)','The client accused Tom of ignoring their emails.'],['to blame (sb for sth)','reprocher (qch à qn), tenir pour responsable','Don’t blame Tom for the delay.'],['to apologise (for doing)','s’excuser (d’avoir fait)','He apologised for keeping them waiting.'],['to insist (on doing)','insister (pour faire)','She insisted on paying for dinner.'],['to offer (to do)','proposer (de faire)','Priya offered to check the files.'],['to refuse (to do)','refuser (de faire)','The landlord refused to fix the heating.'],['to remind (sb to do)','rappeler (à qn de faire)','Can you remind me to call the bank?'],['to persuade (sb to do)','persuader, convaincre (qn de faire)','We persuaded her to stay another week.'],['to threaten (to do)','menacer (de faire)','The client threatened to take their business elsewhere.'],['to point out (that…)','faire remarquer (que)','Priya pointed out that Tom had been covering for her.'],['to claim (that…, to do)','affirmer, prétendre','He claims to have read the whole report.'],['to urge (sb to do)','exhorter, presser (qn de faire)','Experts urge drivers to slow down in icy weather.'],['to congratulate (sb on doing)','féliciter (qn d’avoir fait)','They congratulated her on winning the award.']],
  note:'<b>Faux ami :</b> « prétendre » se dit <i>claim</i> ; <i>pretend</i> veut dire « faire semblant ». <i>He claims he was ill</i> = il prétend qu’il était malade ; <i>He pretended to be ill</i> = il a fait semblant d’être malade.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Recall: from French to English',
  p:`<p>Translate into English, using one of the verbs you’ve just learnt. Think about the construction that follows the verb.</p><p><b>Question:</b> How do you say « Elle a nié avoir lu mes messages. » in English?</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('She denied ',['reading','having read','that she had read','that she read','she had read','she read','that she’d read','she’d read'],' my ',['messages','texts','text messages'],'.'),
  why:'<i>deny</i> + <i>-ing</i>: <i>She denied reading my messages</i>, or with the perfect form <i>She denied having read my messages</i>. A that-clause also works: <i>She denied that she had read my messages.</i> Never <i>denied to read</i>.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Verbs followed by a to-infinitive',
  p:`<p>When Priya says <i>“I’ll do it straight after lunch”</i>, you could report it as <i>Priya said that she would do it after lunch</i>. But one well-chosen verb does the job better: <b>Priya offered to check the files.</b> The verb tells the listener what kind of speech act it was: an offer, a promise, a refusal. Each reporting verb, however, comes with its own construction, and that is where most mistakes happen.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Pattern 1: verb + to-infinitive.</b> <i>agree, offer, refuse, promise, threaten, claim, decide, demand.</i><br><i>She <b>refused to sign</b> the contract. He <b>promised not to tell</b> anyone.</i></p><p><b>Pattern 2: verb + object + to-infinitive.</b> <i>warn, remind, persuade, advise, encourage, urge, invite, tell, ask, beg, order, forbid.</i><br><i>She <b>warned us not to miss</b> another deadline. He <b>reminded me to lock</b> the door.</i></p></div>
<div class="idea"><p>The infinitive looks <b>forward</b>: it reports an action that someone will do, or should do, after the words were spoken. To make it negative, put <b>not</b> directly before <i>to</i>: <i>warn somebody <b>not to</b> do something</i>, <i>promise <b>not to</b> do something</i>.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p>“I’ll lend you the money.” → <i>She offered to lend me the money.</i><br>“Don’t forget to call your gran.” → <i>Dad reminded me to call my gran.</i><br>“If you don’t pay, I’ll call the police.” → <i>He threatened to call the police if I didn’t pay.</i><br>“You should really see a doctor.” → <i>She advised me to see a doctor.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> « suggérer à quelqu’un de faire » ne se traduit <b>jamais</b> par <i>suggest somebody to do</i>. <i>She suggested me to leave</i> est faux : dis <i>She suggested (that) I leave</i>, ou <i>She advised me to leave</i>. De même, « promettre à quelqu’un de faire » donne <i>promise to do</i> ou <i>promise somebody that…</i>, pas <i>promise somebody to do</i>. Et « être d’accord pour aider » : <i>He agreed to help</i> (jamais <i>he was agree</i>).</p></div>`,
  audio:'“I’ll lend you the money.” She offered to lend me the money. “Don’t forget to call your gran.” Dad reminded me to call my gran. They warned us not to swim there.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Verbs followed by -ing or a that-clause',
  p:`<p>Tom says: <i>“That’s my fault, I’m afraid. I forgot to send them.”</i> Report it with <i>admit</i>, and the infinitive pattern no longer works: <b>Tom admitted forgetting to send the designs.</b> A second family of verbs takes <b>-ing</b>, often after a preposition, and many verbs can also be followed by a <b>that-clause</b>.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Pattern 3: verb + -ing.</b> <i>admit, deny, suggest, recommend, mention, regret.</i><br><i>He <b>denied ignoring</b> their emails. She <b>suggested taking</b> a break.</i></p><p><b>Pattern 4: verb (+ object) + preposition + -ing.</b> <i>apologise for, insist on, object to, confess to, accuse somebody of, blame somebody for, congratulate somebody on, thank somebody for, criticise somebody for.</i><br><i>She <b>apologised for being</b> late. They <b>accused him of lying</b>.</i></p><p><b>Pattern 5: verb + that-clause.</b> <i>admit, deny, claim, explain, point out, complain, insist, agree, promise, warn</i>, with the usual tense changes of reported speech.<br><i>She <b>pointed out that</b> the budget had already been spent.</i></p></div>
<div class="idea"><p>The -ing form presents the action as a <b>fact</b>, often something already done. To stress that it happened before, use the perfect form: <i>He denied <b>having taken</b> the money</i> means the same as <i>He denied taking the money</i>. After <i>suggest, recommend</i> and <i>insist</i>, a that-clause can take the bare infinitive (rather formal) or <i>should</i>: <i>She suggested that we <b>take</b> / <b>should take</b> the train.</i> In everyday British English you will also hear <i>She suggested that we <b>took</b> the train.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> après une préposition, le verbe est <b>toujours en -ing</b> : <i>insist on paying</i> (pas <i>insist to pay</i>), <i>apologise for being late</i> (pas <i>for to be</i>). On accuse quelqu’un <b>of</b> quelque chose (pas <i>for</i>), mais on le tient pour responsable <b>for</b> : <i>blame somebody for</i>. <i>Apologise</i> n’a pas de complément direct : <i>apologise <b>to</b> somebody <b>for</b> something</i>. Enfin, <i>suggest to do</i> n’existe pas.</p></div>`,
  audio:'“Yes, I forgot.” Tom admitted forgetting to send the designs. “I didn’t ignore your emails.” He denied ignoring their emails. “Let’s take a break.” She suggested taking a break.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'From direct speech to a reporting verb',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> whenever you report what someone said and want to convey their <b>intention</b>, not just their words: in a summary, a message, minutes of a meeting, a story.</p><p><b>Why it works:</b> the reporting verb already contains the function of the sentence (<i>sorry</i> = apology, <i>why don’t we</i> = suggestion, <i>if I were you</i> = advice). So you can drop those words and keep only the content.</p><p><b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li><b>Identify the function</b>: apology, suggestion, refusal, offer, warning, accusation, admission, denial, reminder…</li><li><b>Choose the verb and its pattern</b>: to-infinitive, object + to-infinitive, -ing, preposition + -ing, or that-clause.</li><li><b>Adjust</b> pronouns, tenses (in a that-clause) and time or place words (<i>yesterday → the day before</i>, <i>here → there</i>).</li><li><b>Drop</b> the words that the verb already expresses: <i>sorry, please, why don’t we, I swear</i>.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Report this sentence: “I’m really sorry I shouted at you yesterday, Kate,” said Sam.',
   etapes:[{f:'Function: an apology. Verb: <i>apologise</i>. Pattern: <i>apologise (to somebody) for + -ing</i>.',w:'“I’m sorry” is the signal. The verb <i>apologise</i> replaces it.'},
    {f:'The action: <i>shouted</i> → <i>for shouting</i> (or <i>for having shouted</i>).',w:'After the preposition <i>for</i>, the verb takes -ing.'},
    {f:'Adjust: <i>at you</i> → <i>at her</i>; <i>yesterday</i> → <i>the day before</i> (or <i>the previous day</i>).',w:'Kate is now spoken about, not spoken to, and “yesterday” is no longer yesterday when we report it later.'},
    {f:'Drop <i>I’m really sorry</i>: the verb carries it. Add <i>sincerely</i> only if the tone matters.',w:'A report is shorter than the original, not a word-for-word copy.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>Sam apologised to Kate for shouting at her the day before.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a denial',
  p:`<p>Report the sentence using <i>deny</i>.</p><p>“I didn’t break the window,” said Ben.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the reported sentence, starting with <i>Ben denied…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Ben denied ',['breaking','having broken','that he had broken','that he broke','he had broken','he broke','that he’d broken','he’d broken'],' the window.'),
  why:'<i>Ben denied breaking the window</i> (or <i>having broken</i>, or <i>that he had broken</i>). The negative <i>didn’t</i> disappears: <i>deny</i> already means “say you didn’t”. <i>Ben denied not breaking</i> would mean the opposite.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a warning',
  p:`<p>Report the sentence using <i>warn</i>.</p><p>“Don’t touch those cables!” the electrician said to us.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the reported sentence, starting with <i>The electrician warned us…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('The electrician warned us not to touch ',['those','the'],' cables.'),
  why:'<i>warn</i> + object + <b>not to</b> + verb: <i>The electrician warned us not to touch the cables.</i> The negative imperative <i>Don’t touch</i> becomes <i>not to touch</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a suggestion',
  p:`<p>Report the sentence using <i>suggest</i>. Maria is travelling with you and your friends.</p><p>“Why don’t we take the train instead?” said Maria.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the reported sentence, starting with <i>Maria suggested…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Maria suggested ',['taking the train','that we take the train','that we should take the train','we take the train','we should take the train','that we took the train','we took the train','that they take the train','that they should take the train','they take the train','they should take the train','going by train','travelling by train','that we go by train','that we should go by train','we go by train','we should go by train'],['',' instead'],'.'),
  why:'Two correct patterns: <i>suggest + -ing</i> (<i>Maria suggested taking the train</i>) or <i>suggest that + subject + (should) + verb</i> (<i>Maria suggested that we (should) take the train</i>). Never <i>suggested to take</i> or <i>suggested us to take</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: an accusation',
  p:`<p>Lucy is furious with her brother Mark. She says to him: “You stole my idea!”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Put the words in order to report what Lucy said.</p>`,
  ordre:['Lucy','accused','Mark','of','stealing','her','idea.'],
  why:'<i>accuse</i> + person + <b>of</b> + -ing: <i>Lucy accused Mark of stealing her idea.</i> <i>My idea</i> becomes <i>her idea</i>, because Lucy is no longer the one speaking.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a refusal',
  p:`<p>Report the sentence using <i>refuse</i>.</p><p>“No, I won’t sign the contract, and that’s final,” said the tenant.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the reported sentence, starting with <i>The tenant refused…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('The tenant ',['','flatly ','firmly '],'refused to sign ',['the contract','it'],'.'),
  why:'<i>refuse</i> + to-infinitive: <i>The tenant refused to sign the contract.</i> No <i>not</i>: refusing already means “saying no”. <i>and that’s final</i> can be suggested by an adverb: <i>flatly refused</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a reminder',
  p:`<p>Report the sentence using <i>remind</i>.</p><p>“Remember to back up your files before Friday,” the IT manager told the staff.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the reported sentence, starting with <i>The IT manager reminded…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('The IT manager reminded ',['the staff','staff','everyone','employees','them'],' to back up their files',['',' before Friday',' by Friday'],'.'),
  why:'<i>remind</i> + object + to-infinitive: <i>The IT manager reminded the staff to back up their files before Friday.</i> <i>Your files</i> becomes <i>their files</i>.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Summarising a conversation',
  p:`<p>Imagine a colleague missed Claire’s meeting and asks what happened. You won’t replay every line. A good summary <b>selects</b> the key points, <b>groups</b> them, and uses reporting verbs that capture each person’s intention.</p>
<div class="def"><p>Tools for summarising:</p><ul><li><b>Reporting verbs</b> that sum up an attitude: <i>admit, deny, point out, warn, suggest, agree, insist</i>.</li><li><b>Summarising phrases</b>: <i>She made it clear that…, He went on to say that…, They raised the issue of…, He brought up…, In the end, they agreed to…, The gist of it was that…</i></li><li><b>Tenses</b>: after a past reporting verb, tenses usually move back (<i>is → was, have done → had done, will → would</i>). If the information is still true, you can keep the present: <i>She said the office <b>is</b> closed on Mondays.</i></li><li><b>Time and place</b>: <i>today → that day, tomorrow → the next day, here → there</i>, when the context has changed.</li></ul></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>Claire told the team that the client hadn’t received the final designs. Tom admitted forgetting to send them, but denied ignoring the client’s emails. Priya pointed out that he had been covering for her and suggested setting up a shared calendar. Claire warned them that they could lose the account and advised Tom to apologise to the client in person. In the end, Priya agreed to check the files after lunch.</i></p><p>Five sentences replace the whole conversation, and every verb carries an intention.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> « dire à quelqu’un » se traduit par <i>tell somebody</i>, jamais <i>say somebody</i> : <i>She told me that…</i> ou <i>She said (to me) that…</i>. « Expliquer à quelqu’un » : <i>explain something <b>to</b> somebody</i> (pas <i>explain me</i>). Et <i>according to</i> sert à citer <b>les autres</b> : <i>according to the report</i>. Pour ton propre avis, dis <i>In my opinion</i>, jamais <i>according to me</i>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'Claire told the team that the client hadn’t received the final designs. Tom admitted forgetting to send them, but denied ignoring the client’s emails. Priya pointed out that he had been covering for her and suggested setting up a shared calendar. Claire warned them that they could lose the account and advised Tom to apologise to the client in person. In the end, Priya agreed to check the files after lunch.'},

 {kind:'Écoute', h:'Which summary is accurate?',
  p:`<p>A customer brings a kettle back to a shop. Listen without reading, then choose the summary that reports the conversation most accurately. Pay attention to the reporting verbs: each wrong option misrepresents someone’s intention.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Which summary is the most accurate?</p>`,
  audio:[['Customer','Hi. I bought this kettle here last week, and it’s already stopped working. I’d like my money back, please.'],['Assistant','Oh, I’m sorry about that. I can certainly swap it for a new one, but I’m afraid we don’t normally give refunds on electrical items.'],['Customer','With respect, it lasted six days. I don’t want another one of the same model. I’d like a refund.'],['Assistant','I understand. Let me have a word with my manager and see what I can do.']], cache:true,
  opts:['The customer asked for a replacement, and the assistant refused to give the customer one.','The assistant accused the customer of breaking the kettle and refused to help.','The customer demanded a refund and refused a replacement; the assistant offered to speak to the manager.','The customer admitted losing the receipt, so the assistant suggested a replacement.'], ans:2,
  why:'The customer insists on a refund (<i>I’d like my money back… I’d like a refund</i>) and turns down a replacement (<i>I don’t want another one</i>). The assistant offers to talk to the manager (<i>Let me have a word with my manager</i>). Nobody mentions a receipt, and the assistant never accuses the customer.'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Word stress in reporting verbs',
  p:`<p>Reporting verbs are short and frequent, and they are often misheard when French speakers give every syllable the same weight.</p>
<div class="def"><p>Most two-syllable reporting verbs are stressed on the <b>second</b> syllable, and the first one is reduced to a weak vowel, /ə/ or /ɪ/:</p><ul><li>ad<b>MIT</b>, de<b>NY</b>, sug<b>GEST</b>, ac<b>CUSE</b>, in<b>SIST</b>, re<b>FUSE</b>, per<b>SUADE</b>, com<b>PLAIN</b>, re<b>MIND</b>.</li><li>Longer verbs: recom<b>MEND</b>, a<b>POL</b>ogise, con<b>GRAT</b>ulate.</li><li>Some words change stress between noun and verb: an <b>OB</b>ject / to ob<b>JECT</b>, a <b>PRO</b>test / to pro<b>TEST</b>, <b>RE</b>fuse (rubbish) / to re<b>FUSE</b>.</li></ul></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> ne prononce pas toutes les voyelles pleinement. Dans <i>accuse</i>, le <i>a</i> se réduit à un son très bref, proche de « eu » : /əˈkjuːz/. Dans <i>suggest</i>, la première syllabe est faible : /səˈdʒest/. C’est la syllabe accentuée qui porte le mot : plus forte, plus longue, plus haute.</p></div>`,
  audio:'admit. deny. suggest. accuse. insist. persuade. recommend. apologise. They organised a protest. They protested outside the town hall. I object to that.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Repeat: stress and rhythm',
  p:`<p>Listen, then say the sentence. Stress the strong syllables and let the others shrink: <i>He ad<b>MIT</b>ted the <b>MIS</b>take, but he de<b>NIED</b> ig<b>NOR</b>ing their <b>E</b>mails.</i></p>`,
  dire:'He admitted the mistake, but he denied ignoring their emails.',
  note:'If a word stays red, listen to it again with « Plus lentement », then try again. The weak first syllables (<i>ad-, de-, ig-</i>) should be quick and light.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'A heated council meeting',
  p:`<p>Read this article from a local newspaper. You can also listen to it. Notice how the journalist uses reporting verbs to show each person’s position without quoting everything.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="The Harbridge Gazette"><p lang="en"><b>Cycle lane plan splits Harbridge</b></p><p lang="en">Plans for a two-mile cycle lane along Harbridge High Street ran into fierce opposition on Tuesday night, when more than a hundred residents packed into the town hall.</p><p lang="en">Councillor Ruth Adeyemi, who has led the project, insisted that the lane would make the town centre safer and pointed out that cycling accidents on the High Street had doubled in five years. She urged residents to look at the evidence rather than “rumours on social media”.</p><p lang="en">Shop owners were not convinced. Speaking on behalf of the High Street Traders’ Association, Mark Ellis accused the council of ignoring local businesses and warned that removing forty parking spaces could force several shops to close. He claimed that the number of shoppers had already fallen by fifteen per cent since roadworks began in the spring. Asked whether the association had been consulted, he denied ever receiving an invitation to the planning workshops.</p><p lang="en">Ms Adeyemi admitted that communication “could have been better”, but refused to scrap the scheme. Instead, she offered to meet traders individually before the final vote and suggested introducing two hours of free parking in the nearby Station Road car park.</p><p lang="en">Not everyone in the hall was against the plan. Several parents complained that their children were too frightened to cycle to school, and one local GP recommended building the lane “as soon as humanly possible”.</p><p lang="en">After almost three hours of debate, the council agreed to postpone the decision until March, when an independent report on traffic and trade is due to be published. Residents who wish to comment have been reminded to submit their views online by 15 February.</p></div>
<p><b>Question:</b> Which statement best describes Councillor Adeyemi’s response to the criticism?</p>`,
  audio:'Cycle lane plan splits Harbridge. Plans for a two-mile cycle lane along Harbridge High Street ran into fierce opposition on Tuesday night, when more than a hundred residents packed into the town hall. Councillor Ruth Adeyemi, who has led the project, insisted that the lane would make the town centre safer and pointed out that cycling accidents on the High Street had doubled in five years. She urged residents to look at the evidence rather than rumours on social media. Shop owners were not convinced. Speaking on behalf of the High Street Traders’ Association, Mark Ellis accused the council of ignoring local businesses and warned that removing forty parking spaces could force several shops to close. He claimed that the number of shoppers had already fallen by fifteen per cent since roadworks began in the spring. Asked whether the association had been consulted, he denied ever receiving an invitation to the planning workshops. Ms Adeyemi admitted that communication could have been better, but refused to scrap the scheme. Instead, she offered to meet traders individually before the final vote and suggested introducing two hours of free parking in the nearby Station Road car park. Not everyone in the hall was against the plan. Several parents complained that their children were too frightened to cycle to school, and one local GP recommended building the lane as soon as humanly possible. After almost three hours of debate, the council agreed to postpone the decision until March, when an independent report on traffic and trade is due to be published. Residents who wish to comment have been reminded to submit their views online by 15 February.',
  opts:['She abandoned the scheme after hearing the shop owners’ concerns.','She acknowledged poor communication but kept the scheme and proposed compromises.','She denied that there had been any problem with communication.','She accused the shop owners of spreading rumours on social media.'], ans:1,
  why:'She <i>admitted that communication could have been better</i> (an acknowledgement) but <i>refused to scrap the scheme</i>, and she <i>offered to meet traders</i> and <i>suggested</i> free parking: compromises. The remark about rumours was addressed to residents in general, not an accusation against the traders.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Reporting the outcome',
  p:`<p>Look again at the last paragraph of the article about Harbridge.</p><p><b>Question:</b> In one sentence, report what the council decided at the end of the meeting. Start with <i>The council agreed…</i></p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('The council agreed to ',['postpone','delay','put off','defer'],' the decision',['',' until March',' till March'],'.').concat(V('The council agreed to put the decision off',['',' until March',' till March'],'.')),
  why:'<i>agree</i> + to-infinitive: <i>The council agreed to postpone the decision until March.</i> <i>Put off</i> or <i>delay</i> are fine too. Note that <i>agree</i> here means “decide together”, not “approve the plan”.'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: an apology',
  p:'Listen and write the sentence exactly. Use British spelling. You can listen as many times as you like, and use « Plus lentement ».',
  dictee:'She apologised for keeping us waiting and offered to drive us home.',
  why:'Two patterns in one sentence: <i>apologise for + -ing</i> and <i>offer + to-infinitive</i>. British spelling: <i>apologised</i> (American: <i>apologized</i>).'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Report a disagreement',
  p:`<p>Think of a disagreement you witnessed or took part in: at work, at home, among friends, or in the news. Speak for about a minute. Say who was involved, what each person said, and how it ended. Use <b>at least four different reporting verbs</b>, and try not to use <i>said</i> at all. Tap « Parler », then « J’ai fini ».</p>`,
  oral:{modele:'Last month, my flatmates and I had a row about cleaning. Julie complained that she was the only one who ever cleaned the kitchen, and she accused Marco of leaving his dishes in the sink for days. Marco denied leaving them for days, but he admitted that he sometimes forgot. I pointed out that we had never actually agreed on any rules, and I suggested drawing up a rota. At first Julie refused to sign up to anything, but in the end she agreed to try it for a month. Marco promised to wash up every evening, and so far he’s kept his word.',
   criteres:['I described the situation and the people involved','I used at least four different reporting verbs (complain, accuse, deny, admit, suggest…)','I used the right construction after each verb (-ing, to-infinitive, object + to, that…)','I said how the disagreement ended','I avoided repeating said']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write up a residents’ meeting',
  p:`<p>Last night you went to a residents’ meeting in your block of flats. Your neighbour Sophie couldn’t come. Here are your notes:</p>
<ul><li><b>Mrs Patel (2nd floor):</b> “The music from flat 6 goes on until 2 a.m. I can’t sleep!”</li><li><b>Dan (flat 6):</b> “It wasn’t me last Saturday, I was away. But yes, I sometimes play music late. Sorry, everyone.”</li><li><b>Building manager:</b> “If it continues, the landlord may issue formal warnings.”</li><li><b>Dan:</b> “I’ll use headphones after 11.”</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> “Let’s have quiet hours from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m.”</li><li><b>Mrs Patel:</b> “Fine, but I want it in writing.”</li><li><b>Manager:</b> “I’ll send everyone a copy by Friday. And please report problems to me, not on the group chat.”</li></ul>
<p>Write an email to Sophie (<b>about 180 words</b>) summarising the meeting. Don’t quote: use reporting verbs with the right constructions, and say what was decided.</p>`,
  ecrire:{min:150, modele:'Hi Sophie,<br><br>You missed quite a lively meeting last night, so here’s a quick summary.<br><br>Mrs Patel complained that the music from flat 6 often went on until 2 a.m. and that she couldn’t sleep. Dan, who lives in flat 6, denied playing music last Saturday, since he had been away, but he admitted that he sometimes played it late and apologised for disturbing everyone.<br><br>The building manager warned us that the landlord might issue formal warnings if the noise continued. Dan then offered to use headphones after 11 p.m., which seemed to calm things down a little.<br><br>In the end, everyone agreed to respect quiet hours from 11 p.m. to 7 a.m. Mrs Patel insisted on having the rule in writing, so the manager promised to send everyone a copy by Friday. He also reminded us to report any problems to him directly rather than complaining on the residents’ group chat.<br><br>All in all, it went much better than I expected, and I think things will be quieter from now on. Let me know if you have any questions!<br><br>See you soon,<br>Alex',
   criteres:[{t:'I used a verb + -ing pattern (deny, admit, suggest, apologise for, insist on…)', re:'\\b(admitted|denied|suggested|recommended|apologi[sz]ed for|insisted on|accused \\w+ of|blamed \\w+ for)\\s+\\w+ing\\b'},{t:'I used a verb + object + to-infinitive (warn, remind, urge, persuade… somebody to)', re:'\\b(warned|reminded|urged|persuaded|advised|encouraged|asked|told)\\s+\\w+(\\s+\\w+)?\\s+(not\\s+)?to\\s+\\w+'},{t:'I used offer, refuse, agree or promise + to-infinitive', re:'\\b(offered|refused|agreed|promised|threatened)\\s+(not\\s+)?to\\s+\\w+'},{t:'I used a that-clause after a reporting verb', re:'\\b(complained|admitted|pointed out|explained|claimed|insisted|warned \\w+)\\s+that\\b'},'I reported the outcome: what was decided and who will do what','I opened and closed the email in a friendly, natural way']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'What to remember',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li>A reporting verb conveys the <b>intention</b> (apology, warning, offer…), so you can drop <i>sorry, please, why don’t we</i>.</li><li><b>Verb + to-infinitive</b>: <i>agree, offer, refuse, promise, threaten, claim</i>. <b>Verb + object + to-infinitive</b>: <i>warn, remind, persuade, advise, urge, encourage, invite</i>. Negative: <i>warn somebody <b>not to</b> do</i>.</li><li><b>Verb + -ing</b>: <i>admit, deny, suggest, recommend</i>. <b>Preposition + -ing</b>: <i>apologise for, insist on, accuse somebody of, blame somebody for, congratulate somebody on</i>.</li><li><b>That-clause</b>: <i>admit, deny, claim, explain, point out, complain, warn</i>. <i>suggest/recommend/insist that + subject + (should) + verb</i>.</li><li>Traps: <i>suggest somebody to do</i>, <i>insist to do</i>, <i>accuse for</i>, <i>explain me</i>, <i>say me</i>, <i>according to me</i> are all wrong.</li><li>Summarise: select, group, use verbs and phrases such as <i>made it clear that, went on to say, in the end they agreed to</i>. Tenses move back unless the fact is still true.</li><li>Stress: ad<b>MIT</b>, de<b>NY</b>, sug<b>GEST</b>, ac<b>CUSE</b>, recom<b>MEND</b>; an <b>OB</b>ject / to ob<b>JECT</b>.</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A voicemail from the landlord',
  p:'You share a flat, and the boiler has been broken for three days. Your landlord leaves a voicemail while your flatmate is out. Listen, then answer the questions one by one. You’ll need to pass the message on accurately.',
  audio:'Hi, it’s Graham, your landlord. Sorry I didn’t get back to you sooner about the boiler. I know it’s been three days without hot water, and that’s not acceptable, so I’ve booked an engineer for Thursday morning, between eight and ten. If that time doesn’t suit you, I can try to move it to Friday. One more thing: please don’t try to restart the boiler yourselves. The last time someone did that, it cost me four hundred pounds. Oh, and don’t forget the rent’s due on the first, so please don’t leave it till the last minute again. Thanks. Bye.', cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) Which summary of the beginning of the message is accurate?', opts:['Graham denied that there was any problem with the boiler.','Graham apologised for not replying sooner and admitted that the situation was unacceptable.','Graham accused the tenants of breaking the boiler.','Graham refused to send an engineer before Friday.'], ans:1, hint:'Listen to the first two sentences: what is he sorry about, and what does he accept?', why:'<i>Sorry I didn’t get back to you sooner</i> = an apology; <i>that’s not acceptable</i> = he admits the situation is unacceptable. He books an engineer for Thursday, with Friday as an alternative.'},
   {q:'b) Report his warning about the boiler. Start with <i>He warned us…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('He warned us not to ',['try to restart','try and restart','restart','try restarting'],' the boiler',['',' ourselves',' by ourselves',' on our own'],'.'), hint:'<i>warn</i> + object + <i>not to</i> + verb.', why:'<i>He warned us not to try to restart the boiler ourselves.</i> <i>Yourselves</i> becomes <i>ourselves</i>.'},
   {q:'c) Report his offer about the appointment, using <i>offer</i>. Start with <i>He offered…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('He offered to ',['','try to '],['move','change','rearrange','reschedule'],' ',['it','the appointment','the visit'],' to Friday',['',' if Thursday didn’t suit us',' if that time didn’t suit us',' if Thursday wasn’t convenient'],'.'), hint:'<i>offer</i> is followed by a to-infinitive, with no object.', why:'<i>He offered to (try to) move the appointment to Friday if Thursday didn’t suit us.</i> Not <i>offered us to move</i>.'},
   {q:'d) Report his last point about the rent, using <i>remind</i>. Start with <i>He reminded us…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('He reminded us ',['that the rent was due','the rent was due','that the rent is due','the rent is due','to pay the rent','not to leave the rent','not to leave it','not to leave paying the rent'],['',' on the first',' on the first of the month',' on the 1st',' till the last minute',' until the last minute',' on time',' by the first'],'.'), hint:'Two patterns are possible: <i>remind somebody that…</i> or <i>remind somebody (not) to do</i>.', why:'<i>He reminded us that the rent was due on the first</i>, or <i>He reminded us not to leave the rent till the last minute.</i> With <i>that</i>, the present <i>is due</i> is also fine, since it’s still true.'}],
  sol:'<p>Graham apologised for not getting back to us sooner and admitted that three days without hot water was unacceptable. He has booked an engineer for Thursday between 8 and 10 a.m., and offered to move the appointment to Friday if Thursday didn’t suit us. He warned us not to try to restart the boiler ourselves, pointing out that it had cost him £400 the last time. Finally, he reminded us that the rent was due on the first and asked us not to leave it till the last minute.</p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'Minutes of a tense meeting',
  p:'You are the secretary of a local tennis club. At the committee meeting, there is a heated discussion about the membership fee. Listen carefully: afterwards you must write accurate minutes, so each person’s intention matters. Answer the questions one by one.',
  audio:[['Helen','Okay, item three: the membership fee. Simon, you wanted to say something.'],['Simon','Yes. Frankly, raising the fee by thirty per cent is outrageous. You’re pricing out families, and you decided it without asking anyone.'],['Helen','That’s not quite fair. We discussed it at the last two meetings. But I accept we could have explained it better in the newsletter.'],['Simon','Then why not phase it in? Ten per cent this year, ten next year, and so on.'],['Ana','I see the logic, but if we don’t raise it by at least twenty per cent this year, we can’t repair the changing rooms. And if they’re not repaired, the council could close them.'],['Helen','How about this: twenty per cent now, and a reduced rate for families with more than one child?'],['Simon','I could live with that, as long as it’s reviewed next year.'],['Helen','Agreed. Ana, could you draft the new rates by Friday?'],['Ana','Will do.']], cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) What did Simon accuse the committee of?', opts:['Spending the club’s money on the changing rooms.','Deciding on the increase without consulting the members.','Hiding the fee increase in the newsletter.','Refusing to discuss the fee at previous meetings.'], ans:1, hint:'Listen to the end of Simon’s first turn.', why:'<i>You decided it without asking anyone</i>: Simon accused the committee of deciding without consulting members. Helen replies that it was discussed at the last two meetings.'},
   {q:'b) Report Helen’s reaction to the criticism, using <i>admit</i>. Start with <i>Helen admitted…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Helen admitted ',['that ',''],['they','the committee','we'],' could have explained it better',['',' in the newsletter'],'.').concat(V('Helen admitted that ',['the increase','the decision','it'],' could have been explained better',['',' in the newsletter'],'.')), hint:'Use a that-clause; <i>could have explained</i> stays as it is.', why:'<i>Helen admitted that they could have explained it better in the newsletter.</i> <i>Could have</i> doesn’t change in reported speech. She also denied that the decision had been taken without discussion.'},
   {q:'c) Report Simon’s proposal, using <i>suggest</i>. Start with <i>Simon suggested…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Simon suggested ',['phasing it in','phasing in the increase','phasing the increase in','phasing in the rise','phasing the rise in','that they phase it in','that they should phase it in','they phase it in','they should phase it in','that the committee phase it in','that the committee should phase it in','that they phase in the increase','that they should phase in the increase','introducing it gradually','introducing the increase gradually','that they introduce it gradually','that they should introduce it gradually','raising the fee gradually','raising it gradually'],['',' over several years',' over a few years',' gradually'],'.'), hint:'<i>suggest</i> + -ing, or <i>suggest that</i> + subject + (should) + verb.', why:'<i>Simon suggested phasing it in</i> (or <i>suggested that they (should) phase it in</i>) over several years: ten per cent a year. Never <i>suggested to phase</i>.'},
   {q:'d) Report Ana’s warning, using <i>warn</i>. Start with <i>Ana warned…</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Ana warned ',['that ',''],['the council could close the changing rooms','the council might close the changing rooms','the changing rooms could be closed','the changing rooms might be closed','the council could close them'],['',' if they were not repaired',' if they weren’t repaired',' unless they were repaired'],'.').concat(V('Ana warned ',['that ',''],'if the changing rooms were not repaired, the council could close them.')), hint:'<i>warn that</i> + a clause, with the tense moved back: <i>are not → were not</i>.', why:'<i>Ana warned that the council could close the changing rooms if they weren’t repaired.</i> She also pointed out that a rise of at least twenty per cent was needed this year.'},
   {q:'e) Which sentence best records the outcome in the minutes?', opts:['The committee agreed to a 30% rise, phased in over three years.','Simon refused to accept any increase until next year.','The committee agreed to a 20% rise with a reduced rate for larger families, to be reviewed next year; Ana agreed to draft the new rates by Friday.','Helen insisted on a 20% rise and threatened to close the changing rooms.'], ans:2, hint:'Listen to Helen’s compromise and Simon’s condition.', why:'Helen proposes 20% plus a family discount, Simon accepts <i>as long as it’s reviewed next year</i>, and Ana agrees to draft the rates by Friday.'}],
  sol:'<p><b>Minutes, item 3: membership fee.</b> Simon accused the committee of deciding on a 30% increase without consulting members and warned that it would price out families. Helen pointed out that the issue had been discussed at the last two meetings, but admitted that it could have been explained better in the newsletter. Simon suggested phasing the increase in at 10% a year. Ana warned that without a rise of at least 20% this year the changing rooms could not be repaired, and that the council could close them. Helen proposed a 20% rise with a reduced rate for families with more than one child. Simon accepted, provided that the fee is reviewed next year. Ana agreed to draft the new rates by Friday.</p>'},
]},
/* ===================================================================== */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-argument', o:8, h:'Making a case', p:'Argue convincingly: concede and contrast with <i>although, despite, whereas, however, nevertheless</i>, then build a structured argumentative essay with a thesis, counter-arguments and a conclusion.', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'Cars out of the city centre?',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you’ll be able to build a convincing argument in speech and in writing: conceding a point, contrasting ideas with the right linkers, and organising a structured argumentative essay.</p>
<p>A local radio station hosts a short debate. Jenny Cole campaigns for safer cycling; Martin Hughes runs a delivery firm. <b>Listen without reading</b> first, twice if you like.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is Martin’s position on banning cars from the city centre?</p>`,
  audio:[['Host','Good evening. Tonight’s question: should Brayford ban private cars from its city centre? With me are Jenny Cole, who campaigns for safer cycling, and Martin Hughes, who runs a local delivery firm. Jenny, you first.'],['Jenny','Thanks. Look, the case is simple. Air quality in the centre is among the worst in the region, and whereas other cities have acted, we’ve done next to nothing. A ban would make the streets safer and quieter almost overnight.'],['Host','Martin, you’re not convinced.'],['Martin','Actually, I’m not against it in principle. Although my business depends on vans, I can see the centre is choked with traffic. However, a ban without decent public transport would hit older people and small shops hardest. Despite what some campaigners say, not everyone can hop on a bike.'],['Jenny','Nobody’s saying they should. But we can’t keep putting off change just because it’s inconvenient.'],['Martin','Fair enough. Even so, I’d want buses running every ten minutes before a single street is closed.']], cache:true,
  opts:['He opposes any restriction on cars, because his business depends on vans.','He thinks a ban would help small shops and older people.','He wants an immediate ban, as Jenny does.','He accepts the idea in principle, but wants good public transport in place first.'], ans:3,
  why:'Martin says <i>I’m not against it in principle</i>, concedes that the centre is <i>choked with traffic</i>, then objects: <i>However, a ban without decent public transport would hit older people and small shops hardest.</i> His condition: <i>buses running every ten minutes before a single street is closed</i>.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'The language of argument',
  p:`<p>These expressions help you present, weigh and defend ideas, in a debate as well as in an essay. Tap a line to hear it, and notice the prepositions: they are part of the expression.</p>`,
  mots:[['to make a case (for sth)','défendre, plaider (pour qch)','She made a strong case for a four-day week.'],['a valid point','un argument recevable, une remarque juste','That’s a valid point, but it doesn’t change my mind.'],['to raise a concern','soulever une inquiétude','Several parents raised concerns about safety.'],['to weigh up the pros and cons','peser le pour et le contre','We need to weigh up the pros and cons before deciding.'],['to outweigh','l’emporter sur','The benefits far outweigh the risks.'],['a drawback','un inconvénient','The main drawback is the cost.'],['to address (a problem)','traiter, s’attaquer à (un problème)','The report fails to address the real issue.'],['to back up (a claim)','étayer (une affirmation)','Can you back that up with figures?'],['evidence','les preuves, les données (indénombrable)','There is little evidence that it works.'],['to some extent','dans une certaine mesure','I agree with you to some extent.'],['admittedly','certes, il faut le reconnaître','Admittedly, the plan is expensive.'],['it could be argued that…','on pourrait soutenir que…','It could be argued that tourism does more harm than good.'],['to strike a balance (between)','trouver un équilibre (entre)','We need to strike a balance between freedom and safety.'],['to come at a cost','avoir un prix, se payer cher','Convenience often comes at a cost.'],['a compelling argument','un argument convaincant','There is a compelling argument for change.'],['to play devil’s advocate','se faire l’avocat du diable','Let me play devil’s advocate for a moment.'],['to take (sth) into account','prendre (qch) en compte','The plan doesn’t take older people into account.'],['to jump to conclusions','tirer des conclusions hâtives','Let’s not jump to conclusions.']],
  note:'<b>Faux ami :</b> <i>evidence</i> = les preuves, les données, et il est <b>indénombrable</b> : <i>some evidence, a piece of evidence</i>, jamais <i>an evidence</i> ni <i>evidences</i>. « C’est une évidence » se dit <i>It’s obvious</i> ou <i>It goes without saying</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Recall: weighing it up',
  p:`<p>Translate into English, using an expression from the list.</p><p><b>Question:</b> How do you say « Les avantages l’emportent largement sur les inconvénients. » in English?</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('The ',['advantages','benefits','pros'],' ',['far ','greatly ','clearly ','largely ','easily ','by far ',''],'outweigh the ',['disadvantages','drawbacks','cons'],'.'),
  why:'<i>The advantages far outweigh the disadvantages.</i> <i>outweigh</i> is a transitive verb, with no preposition: not <i>outweigh on</i>. To intensify it, use <i>far</i> or <i>greatly</i>.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Although, even though, despite, in spite of',
  p:`<p>Martin says: <i>“Although my business depends on vans, I can see the centre is choked with traffic.”</i> He <b>concedes</b> a point (his business needs vans), but it doesn’t change his conclusion. Concession is the backbone of a convincing argument, and English has two families of words to express it, with different grammar.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>although, even though, though</b> + a clause (subject + verb):<br><i><b>Although it was raining</b>, we went out.</i></p><p><b>despite, in spite of</b> + a noun, a pronoun or an -ing form:<br><i><b>Despite the rain</b>, we went out. <b>In spite of feeling</b> tired, she finished the report.</i></p><p>To use a full clause after <i>despite</i>, add <b>the fact that</b>: <i>Despite the fact that it was raining, we went out.</i></p></div>
<div class="idea"><p><i>Even though</i> is stronger than <i>although</i>: it underlines the surprise (<i>Even though I’d warned him twice, he went ahead</i>). <i>Though</i> is more informal and can also come at the end of a sentence: <i>It’s expensive. I love it, though.</i> The concession can come first, followed by a comma, or second: <i>We went out although it was raining.</i></p></div>
<div class="ex"><p>One idea, four correct versions:<br><i>Although the hotel was expensive, we enjoyed our stay.<br>Even though the hotel was expensive, we enjoyed our stay.<br>Despite the high price of the hotel, we enjoyed our stay.<br>In spite of the fact that the hotel was expensive, we enjoyed our stay.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> « malgré » = <i>despite</i>, <b>sans</b> <i>of</i> : <i>despite of</i> est faux, alors que <i>in spite</i> exige <i>of</i>. « Bien que » est suivi du subjonctif, mais <i>although</i> prend un verbe ordinaire : <i>although he <b>is</b> rich</i>. <i>Although the rain</i> est faux : <i>although</i> doit être suivi d’un sujet et d’un verbe. Et n’ajoute jamais <i>but</i> après une proposition en <i>although</i> : <i>Although it was late, <s>but</s> we stayed.</i></p></div>`,
  audio:'Although it was raining, we went out. Despite the rain, we went out. In spite of feeling tired, she finished the report. Even though I’d warned him twice, he went ahead.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Whereas, while, however, nevertheless',
  p:`<p>Jenny says: <i>“Whereas other cities have acted, we’ve done next to nothing.”</i> Nothing is conceded here: two facts are simply <b>contrasted</b>. And when Martin says <i>“However, a ban without decent public transport would hit older people…”</i>, he links his sentence to the previous one. These are two more tools, again with their own grammar.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>whereas, while</b> + a clause: they contrast two facts <b>within one sentence</b>.<br><i>Jenny wants an immediate ban, <b>whereas</b> Martin wants buses first.</i></p><p><b>however, nevertheless, nonetheless, even so, on the other hand, by contrast</b>: adverbs that link <b>two sentences</b>. They come after a full stop or a semicolon and are followed by a comma.<br><i>The plan is popular. <b>However,</b> it is expensive.</i> / <i>The plan is popular; <b>however,</b> it is expensive.</i> <i>However</i> can also sit inside the sentence: <i>The plan, however, is expensive.</i></p></div>
<div class="idea"><p><i>However</i> is a neutral contrast, the formal equivalent of <i>but</i>. <i>Nevertheless, nonetheless</i> and <i>even so</i> mean “despite this, still”: the second fact stands in spite of the first. <i>On the other hand</i> presents the other side of a question, often after <i>on the one hand</i>. <i>Yet</i> works like <i>but</i>, with a note of surprise: <i>a simple yet effective idea</i>.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>Trains are faster; buses, on the other hand, are cheaper.</i><br><i>The evidence is limited. Nevertheless, the results are encouraging.</i><br><i>Older residents rely on cars, while younger ones increasingly cycle.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> « en revanche, par contre » ne se traduit pas par <i>on the contrary</i>. <i>On the contrary</i> sert seulement à contredire ce qui précède : <i>It wasn’t boring. On the contrary, it was fascinating.</i> Pour opposer deux faits, dis <i>on the other hand, by contrast</i> ou <i>whereas</i>. Autre piège : <i>however</i> n’est pas une conjonction. <i>It’s cheap, however it’s slow</i> est faux : écris <i>It’s cheap, but it’s slow</i> ou <i>It’s cheap; however, it’s slow.</i> Enfin, « tandis que » = <i>whereas, while</i>, jamais <i>during</i> (suivi d’un nom).</p></div>`,
  audio:'Jenny wants an immediate ban, whereas Martin wants buses first. The plan is popular. However, it is expensive. The evidence is limited. Nevertheless, the results are encouraging.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Switching between contrast linkers',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> to vary your style in an essay, to avoid repeating <i>but</i>, or in a rewriting exercise where you must keep the meaning with a different linker.</p><p><b>Why it works:</b> <i>although, despite</i> and <i>however</i> express the same relation (a fact that doesn’t prevent another), but each one requires a different grammatical shape after it: a clause, a noun or -ing form, or a new sentence.</p><p><b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li><b>Find the conceded fact</b> and its shape (usually subject + verb).</li><li><b>Check what the new linker needs</b>: <i>although</i> + clause; <i>despite / in spite of</i> + noun, -ing form, or <i>the fact that</i> + clause; <i>however / nevertheless</i> + a separate sentence.</li><li><b>Reshape</b> the fact: turn the clause into a noun phrase (<i>it was expensive → the high price</i>) or an -ing form (<i>she was tired → being tired</i>), or split into two sentences.</li><li><b>Check</b> punctuation and the traps: no <i>of</i> after <i>despite</i>, a comma after <i>However</i>, no <i>but</i> after <i>although</i>.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Rewrite in two ways, keeping the meaning: “Although she had trained for months, she didn’t finish the marathon.” (1) with <i>despite</i>; (2) with <i>however</i>.',
   etapes:[{f:'Conceded fact: <i>she had trained for months</i> (subject + verb).',w:'This is the part that “should” have led to a different result.'},
    {f:'(1) <i>despite</i> needs a noun or an -ing form: <i>Despite <b>having trained</b> for months, she didn’t finish the marathon.</i> Or: <i>Despite <b>months of training</b>, …</i>',w:'The subject disappears in the -ing version because it is the same as in the main clause (<i>she</i>).'},
    {f:'Another option: <i>Despite <b>the fact that</b> she had trained for months, she didn’t finish the marathon.</i>',w:'<i>the fact that</i> lets you keep the full clause. It is correct, but heavier.'},
    {f:'(2) <i>however</i> links two sentences: <i>She had trained for months. <b>However,</b> she didn’t finish the marathon.</i>',w:'Full stop (or semicolon) before, comma after.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>Despite having trained for months, she didn’t finish the marathon.</i> / <i>She had trained for months. However, she didn’t finish the marathon.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: from although to despite',
  p:`<p>Rewrite the sentence so that it begins with <i>Despite</i>, keeping the meaning.</p><p>“Although she had a lot of experience, she didn’t get the job.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the new sentence.</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Despite ',['her experience','her considerable experience','her extensive experience','her wide experience','her long experience','all her experience','having a lot of experience','having lots of experience','having so much experience','having plenty of experience','her having a lot of experience','the fact that she had a lot of experience','the fact that she had lots of experience'],', she ',['didn’t get','failed to get'],' the job.'),
  why:'<i>despite</i> + noun (<i>Despite her experience</i>) or + -ing (<i>Despite having a lot of experience</i>), or <i>despite the fact that</i> + clause. Never <i>Despite she had</i> or <i>Despite of</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: contrasting two facts',
  p:`<p>Join the two sentences into one, using <i>whereas</i>.</p><p>“In the north, winters are long and dark. In the south, they are mild and sunny.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the new sentence.</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('In the north, winters are long and dark, whereas in the south ',['they are','winters are','they’re'],' mild and sunny.').concat(V('Whereas in the north winters are long and dark, in the south ',['they are','winters are','they’re'],' mild and sunny.'),V('Winters are long and dark in the north, whereas in the south ',['they are','they’re'],' mild and sunny.'),['Winters are long and dark in the north, whereas they are mild and sunny in the south.','In the north, winters are long and dark, whereas they are mild and sunny in the south.']),
  why:'<i>whereas</i> + a clause contrasts two facts in one sentence: <i>In the north, winters are long and dark, whereas in the south they are mild and sunny.</i> A comma usually comes before <i>whereas</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: the right linker',
  p:`<p>Complete with a linker that means “despite this” and starts the second sentence.</p><p>“The new law is deeply unpopular. ___, the government intends to go ahead with it.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the missing linker (one word or a short phrase).</p>`,
  lab:'The linker', txt:['Nevertheless','Nonetheless','Even so','However','Still','That said','Despite this','In spite of this','Despite that','In spite of that','All the same','Yet','Regardless'],
  why:'<i>Nevertheless</i>, <i>nonetheless</i> or <i>even so</i> fit best: the government goes ahead <b>despite</b> the unpopularity. <i>However</i> is also correct, but more neutral. Not <i>Although</i> or <i>Despite</i> alone: they can’t start a sentence that stands on its own.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: find and fix',
  p:`<p>This sentence contains a typical mistake.</p><p>“Despite of the bad weather, the festival was a great success.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the corrected sentence.</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V(['Despite','In spite of','Despite all'],' the bad weather, the festival was a great success.').concat(['Although the weather was bad, the festival was a great success.','Even though the weather was bad, the festival was a great success.','Despite the fact that the weather was bad, the festival was a great success.']),
  why:'<i>despite</i> takes no <i>of</i>: <i>Despite the bad weather…</i> If you prefer to keep <i>of</i>, you need <i>in spite of</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: build a concession',
  p:`<p>In a debate, you want to concede a point before defending your position.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Put the words in order to form the sentence.</p>`,
  ordre:['Admittedly,','the','scheme','is','expensive,','but','the','benefits','outweigh','the','costs.'],
  why:'<i>Admittedly</i> opens the concession (“I accept that…”), <i>but</i> brings the rebuttal: <i>Admittedly, the scheme is expensive, but the benefits outweigh the costs.</i>'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'How an argumentative essay is built',
  p:`<p>A strong opinion is not yet an essay. The reader, often an examiner or a decision-maker, needs to follow your reasoning, see that you have considered other views, and reach a clear conclusion. That is why argumentative essays in English follow a fairly predictable structure.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Four parts</b>, each in its own paragraph:</p><ol><li><b>Introduction</b>: a sentence of context, then your <b>thesis</b>, the position you will defend: <i>I believe that a tourist tax is a fair and sensible measure.</i></li><li><b>Body paragraph(s)</b>: each one starts with a <b>topic sentence</b> (the main idea), then an explanation and an example or evidence.</li><li><b>Counter-argument</b>: present the opposing view fairly, concede what is valid, then rebut it.</li><li><b>Conclusion</b>: restate your position in new words and weigh up the arguments. <b>No new argument</b> here.</li></ol></div>
<div class="idea"><p>Conceding a point makes you <b>more</b> convincing, not less: it shows that you have weighed both sides. The register is formal: no contractions, few personal pronouns, and <b>hedging</b> to avoid overclaiming: <i>tends to, is likely to, may, arguably, it seems that</i>. Signposts guide the reader: <i>Firstly, Furthermore, Moreover, In addition, Finally, In conclusion, On balance.</i></p></div>
<div class="ex"><p>Topic sentence: <i>Firstly, mass tourism comes at a cost to residents.</i><br>Hedged claim: <i>A small daily charge is unlikely to deter most visitors.</i><br>Conclusion: <i>On balance, the benefits of the tax clearly outweigh its drawbacks.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones, faux amis fréquents :</b> <i>actually</i> = en fait (pas « actuellement », qui se dit <i>currently, at present</i>) ; <i>eventually</i> = finalement, au bout du compte (pas « éventuellement », qui se dit <i>possibly</i>) ; <i>at first</i> = au début, dans un récit (pour « d’abord » dans un essai : <i>firstly, first of all</i>). Et évite d’enchaîner les <i>I think</i> : varie avec <i>It seems clear that…, There is little doubt that…, It could be argued that…</i></p></div>`},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Writing a concession paragraph',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> in the counter-argument paragraph of an essay, or whenever you answer an objection in a debate.</p><p><b>Why it works:</b> the reader sees that you know the other side and have a reason to reject it. A rebuttal without a concession sounds stubborn; a concession without a rebuttal weakens your own thesis.</p><p><b>How</b>, in four moves:</p>
<ol><li><b>State the opposing view</b> fairly: <i>Opponents argue that…, Critics claim that…, It is often said that…</i></li><li><b>Concede</b> what is valid: <i>Admittedly…, It is true that…, To some extent…</i></li><li><b>Turn</b>: <i>However, Nevertheless, Even so, …</i></li><li><b>Rebut</b> with a reason or evidence, then link back to your thesis, with a hedge if needed.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Thesis of the essay: “Public transport in cities should be free.” Write the counter-argument paragraph.',
   etapes:[{f:'Opposing view: <i>Opponents argue that free public transport would place a heavy burden on taxpayers.</i>',w:'Neutral reporting verb (<i>argue</i>), no mockery: you present the best version of the other side.'},
    {f:'Concession: <i>Admittedly, ticket sales cover a significant share of running costs.</i>',w:'You accept the true part of the objection.'},
    {f:'Turn and rebuttal: <i>Nevertheless, these costs would be partly offset by lower spending on road maintenance and on the health effects of pollution.</i>',w:'<i>Nevertheless</i> signals that your position stands despite the concession; then comes the reason.'},
    {f:'Link back, with a hedge: <i>Free transport may therefore be far less expensive than it first appears.</i>',w:'<i>may</i> and <i>therefore</i>: a cautious conclusion that brings the reader back to the thesis.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>Opponents argue that free public transport would place a heavy burden on taxpayers. Admittedly, ticket sales cover a significant share of running costs. Nevertheless, these costs would be partly offset by lower spending on road maintenance and on the health effects of pollution. Free transport may therefore be far less expensive than it first appears.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a strong conclusion',
  p:`<p>Essay title: “Should homework be banned in primary schools?” The writer’s thesis is that homework should be limited, not banned.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Which is the best concluding paragraph?</p>`,
  opts:['In conclusion, homework should be banned. Another problem is that teachers spend too much time marking it, which is very tiring for them.','So yeah, I think homework is kind of pointless for little kids, but whatever.','On balance, although homework can help children develop good habits, its benefits at this age seem limited. A short, optional amount of reading would therefore strike a better balance than a complete ban.','To sum up, homework is good and bad. Some people like it and some people don’t.'], ans:2,
  why:'The third option restates the thesis in new words (<i>limited, not banned</i>), weighs both sides with a concession (<i>although…</i>), hedges (<i>seem limited</i>) and adds no new argument. The first contradicts the thesis and adds a new point; the second is far too informal; the fourth says nothing.'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Linkers and contrastive stress',
  p:`<p>Linkers are signposts for the listener: if they are mispronounced or swallowed, the logic of your argument gets lost.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li>al<b>THOUGH</b> /ɔːlˈðəʊ/, where<b>AS</b> /weərˈæz/, how<b>EV</b>er /haʊˈevə/, de<b>SPITE</b> /dɪˈspaɪt/, ad<b>MIT</b>tedly /ədˈmɪtɪdli/.</li><li>neverthe<b>LESS</b> /ˌnevəðəˈles/ and nonethe<b>LESS</b> /ˌnʌnðəˈles/: the main stress falls on the <b>last</b> syllable.</li><li><b>Contrastive stress</b>: in a contrast, stress the words that carry the opposition. <i>Jenny wants a ban <b>NOW</b>, whereas Martin wants <b>BUS</b>es first.</i> <i>I’m not against the <b>BAN</b>; I’m against the <b>TIM</b>ing.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> le <i>th</i> de <i>although</i>, <i>nevertheless</i>, <i>nonetheless</i> est sonore /ð/ : la langue entre les dents, en faisant vibrer la voix, ni « z » ni « d ». Et n’accentue pas <i>however</i> sur la première syllabe : how<b>EV</b>er.</p></div>`,
  audio:'although. whereas. however. despite. admittedly. nevertheless. nonetheless. Jenny wants a ban now, whereas Martin wants buses first. I’m not against the ban; I’m against the timing.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Repeat: concede, then defend',
  p:`<p>Listen, then say the two sentences. Pause slightly after each linker, and stress the end of <i>neverthe<b>LESS</b></i>.</p>`,
  dire:'Admittedly, it’s expensive. Nevertheless, I think it’s worth every penny.',
  note:'If <i>nevertheless</i> stays red, practise it in three blocks, <i>never · the · LESS</i>, then join them. The <i>th</i> is soft and voiced.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'The four-day week: too good to be true?',
  p:`<p>Read this opinion piece. You can also listen to it. Pay attention to how the writer moves from one side of the argument to the other.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Opinion"><p lang="en"><b>The four-day week: too good to be true?</b></p><p lang="en">Few workplace ideas have spread as quickly as the four-day week. The principle is simple: staff work four days instead of five, for the same pay, on condition that productivity does not fall. Supporters point to trials in which most participating companies chose to keep the new schedule, citing lower stress, fewer sick days and staff who were far less likely to quit.</p><p lang="en">It is easy to see the appeal. Many employees spend a large part of their week in meetings that achieve little, and a shorter week forces managers to cut what is unnecessary. Moreover, a three-day weekend gives people time to rest, look after their families and pursue interests that make them better at their jobs.</p><p lang="en">Admittedly, the evidence has its limits. Companies that volunteer for trials tend to be those most likely to succeed, and results from offices may say little about hospitals, schools or restaurants, where someone has to be present every day. A nurse cannot simply answer emails more efficiently. Critics also warn that squeezing five days of work into four may simply move the stress from Friday to the rest of the week.</p><p lang="en">Nevertheless, these objections argue for caution rather than rejection. Whereas a rigid national rule would almost certainly fail, flexible schemes designed sector by sector could bring real benefits. Hospitals, for instance, could offer four longer shifts instead of five shorter ones.</p><p lang="en">On balance, the four-day week is neither a miracle nor a fantasy. It is a tool that works well in some workplaces and badly in others. The real question is not whether we should all adopt it tomorrow, but where it makes sense to try.</p></div>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the writer’s overall position?</p>`,
  audio:'The four-day week: too good to be true? Few workplace ideas have spread as quickly as the four-day week. The principle is simple: staff work four days instead of five, for the same pay, on condition that productivity does not fall. Supporters point to trials in which most participating companies chose to keep the new schedule, citing lower stress, fewer sick days and staff who were far less likely to quit. It is easy to see the appeal. Many employees spend a large part of their week in meetings that achieve little, and a shorter week forces managers to cut what is unnecessary. Moreover, a three-day weekend gives people time to rest, look after their families and pursue interests that make them better at their jobs. Admittedly, the evidence has its limits. Companies that volunteer for trials tend to be those most likely to succeed, and results from offices may say little about hospitals, schools or restaurants, where someone has to be present every day. A nurse cannot simply answer emails more efficiently. Critics also warn that squeezing five days of work into four may simply move the stress from Friday to the rest of the week. Nevertheless, these objections argue for caution rather than rejection. Whereas a rigid national rule would almost certainly fail, flexible schemes designed sector by sector could bring real benefits. Hospitals, for instance, could offer four longer shifts instead of five shorter ones. On balance, the four-day week is neither a miracle nor a fantasy. It is a tool that works well in some workplaces and badly in others. The real question is not whether we should all adopt it tomorrow, but where it makes sense to try.',
  opts:['The four-day week should become the law for all workers as soon as possible.','The evidence is so weak that the idea should be abandoned.','The writer refuses to take any position at all.','The idea is worth trying, cautiously and sector by sector, rather than as a single national rule.'], ans:3,
  why:'Paragraph 4 gives the position: <i>these objections argue for caution rather than rejection</i>, and <i>flexible schemes designed sector by sector could bring real benefits</i>, whereas <i>a rigid national rule would almost certainly fail</i>. The conclusion confirms it: <i>where it makes sense to try</i>.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Reading for structure',
  p:`<p>Look again at paragraph 3 of the opinion piece, which begins the counter-argument.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Which single word does the writer use to signal the concession at the start of that paragraph?</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['Admittedly'],
  why:'<i>Admittedly, the evidence has its limits.</i> The writer concedes the critics’ points, then turns with <i>Nevertheless</i> at the start of paragraph 4: a classic concession and rebuttal.'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: a concession',
  p:'Listen and write the sentence exactly. You can listen as many times as you like, and use « Plus lentement ».',
  dictee:'Although the idea sounds appealing, the costs would be considerable.',
  why:'<i>Although</i> + clause, a comma, then the main clause. Watch the spelling: <i>appealing</i> (double p, double l: <i>appeal</i> + <i>-ing</i>), <i>considerable</i>.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Argue your case',
  p:`<p>Speak for one to two minutes on this statement: <b>“Smartphones should be banned in secondary schools.”</b> State your position, give two reasons, concede one point from the other side and rebut it, then conclude. Tap « Parler », then « J’ai fini ».</p>`,
  oral:{modele:'In my view, smartphones should be banned during the school day. First of all, they’re a constant distraction: even when they’re on silent, students keep checking them, which makes it much harder to concentrate. Secondly, a ban would reduce cyberbullying, at least during school hours. Admittedly, phones can be useful for research, and some parents want to be able to contact their children. Nevertheless, schools already have computers for research, and parents can always call the school office in an emergency. So, on balance, I think the benefits of a ban clearly outweigh the drawbacks.',
   criteres:['I stated my position clearly at the start','I gave two reasons, with signposts (first of all, secondly…)','I conceded a point (admittedly, it’s true that…)','I rebutted it (nevertheless, even so, however…)','I concluded by weighing up the arguments (on balance…)']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write an argumentative essay',
  p:`<p>Write a short argumentative essay (<b>about 180 words</b>) on this statement: <b>“Popular tourist cities should make visitors pay a daily tax.”</b></p><p>Use four paragraphs: an introduction with a clear thesis, a body paragraph with your main arguments, a counter-argument paragraph (concession + rebuttal), and a conclusion. Write in a formal register: no contractions.</p>`,
  ecrire:{min:150, modele:'Every summer, cities such as Venice and Barcelona are overwhelmed by visitors. Some have started charging tourists a daily fee, and I believe that this is a fair and sensible measure.<br><br>Firstly, mass tourism comes at a cost. Streets, public transport and rubbish collection are used by millions of people who pay no local taxes, whereas residents cover the bill. A modest daily charge would help to pay for these services. Furthermore, it could fund the protection of historic buildings that are being damaged by overcrowding, and it might encourage some visitors to come outside the peak season.<br><br>Admittedly, critics argue that such a tax would discourage visitors and harm local businesses. Although this concern is understandable, a charge of a few euros seems unlikely to change anyone’s holiday plans. Nevertheless, it should be introduced gradually and its effects monitored carefully.<br><br>In conclusion, despite the risks, a tourist tax appears to be a reasonable way to make visitors contribute to the places they enjoy. The real challenge is not whether to introduce it, but how to spend the money wisely.',
   criteres:[{t:'I used although or even though', re:'\\b(although|even though)\\b'},{t:'I used despite or in spite of', re:'\\b(despite|in spite of)\\b'},{t:'I linked sentences with however, nevertheless, nonetheless or even so', re:'\\b(however|nevertheless|nonetheless|even so)\\b'},{t:'I wrote a conclusion (In conclusion, To sum up, On balance…)', re:'\\b(in conclusion|to sum up|on balance|all things considered|to conclude)\\b'},{t:'I avoided contractions (formal register)', re:'^(?![\\s\\S]*\\w(n[’\']t|[’\'](re|ve|ll|m|d))\\b)'},'I wrote four paragraphs: introduction with a clear thesis, body, counter-argument with a rebuttal, conclusion','Each body paragraph starts with a clear topic sentence','My conclusion adds no new argument']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'Key points',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li><b>Concession</b>: <i>although / even though / though</i> + clause; <i>despite / in spite of</i> + noun or -ing; <i>despite the fact that</i> + clause. Never <i>despite of</i>, never <i>although … but</i>.</li><li><b>Contrast within a sentence</b>: <i>whereas, while</i> + clause.</li><li><b>Linking two sentences</b>: <i>However, Nevertheless, Nonetheless, Even so, On the other hand, By contrast</i>, after a full stop or semicolon, followed by a comma. <i>However</i> is not a conjunction.</li><li><i>On the contrary</i> contradicts; it doesn’t mean « en revanche ».</li><li><b>Switching linkers</b>: find the conceded fact, check what the linker needs (clause, noun/-ing, new sentence), reshape, check punctuation.</li><li><b>Essay</b>: introduction with a thesis; body paragraphs with topic sentences; counter-argument (opposing view → concession → turn → rebuttal); conclusion without new arguments. Formal register, no contractions, hedging (<i>may, tends to, arguably</i>).</li><li>Faux amis : <i>actually</i> = en fait, <i>eventually</i> = finalement, <i>evidence</i> = preuves (indénombrable).</li><li>Stress: al<b>THOUGH</b>, where<b>AS</b>, how<b>EV</b>er, neverthe<b>LESS</b>.</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'Should the office go remote?',
  p:'Your manager, Karen, is discussing a new remote-working policy with two colleagues, Ravi and Lucy. You need to write a short, balanced summary for the rest of the team. Listen, then answer the questions one by one.',
  audio:[['Karen','So, the proposal is to let everyone work from home three days a week. Ravi, Lucy, thoughts?'],['Ravi','I’m all for it. I spend nearly three hours a day commuting. I’d get that time back, and frankly I get more done at home.'],['Lucy','I can see the appeal, but I’m worried about the new starters. They learn so much just by sitting next to experienced people.'],['Ravi','That’s a fair point, I’ll admit. Even so, we could have a fixed office day for training.'],['Lucy','Maybe. But one day a week isn’t the same as overhearing how people handle real problems.'],['Karen','Right. Let’s try a compromise: two days at home rather than three, and new starters in the office full-time for their first three months. We’ll review it in the spring.']], cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) What does Ravi concede?', opts:['That commuting isn’t really a problem for him.','That Lucy has a valid point about the new starters.','That he works less efficiently at home.','That the proposal should be abandoned.'], ans:1, hint:'Listen for <i>I’ll admit</i>.', why:'<i>That’s a fair point, I’ll admit.</i> He concedes Lucy’s point about new starters, then turns with <i>Even so</i> to propose a fixed office day.'},
   {q:'b) Join these two sentences with <i>whereas</i>: “Ravi is all for the proposal. Lucy has doubts about it.”', lab:'Your sentence', txt:['Ravi is all for the proposal, whereas Lucy has doubts about it.','Whereas Ravi is all for the proposal, Lucy has doubts about it.','Ravi is all for the proposal whereas Lucy has doubts about it.'], hint:'<i>whereas</i> + clause, usually after a comma.', why:'<i>Ravi is all for the proposal, whereas Lucy has doubts about it.</i> Two facts contrasted in one sentence.'},
   {q:'c) Rewrite this sentence so that it begins with <i>Despite</i>: “Although Lucy can see the appeal, she is worried about the new starters.”', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Despite ',['seeing the appeal','being able to see the appeal','the appeal','the plan’s appeal','the appeal of the plan','the appeal of the proposal','the proposal’s appeal','the appeal of working from home','the fact that she can see the appeal'],', ',['Lucy','she'],' is worried about ',['the new starters','new starters'],'.').concat(V('Despite the fact that Lucy can see the appeal, she is worried about ',['the new starters','new starters'],'.')), hint:'<i>despite</i> + noun, + -ing, or + <i>the fact that</i>.', why:'<i>Despite seeing the appeal, Lucy is worried about the new starters</i>, or <i>Despite the appeal of the plan…</i> Never <i>Despite she can see</i>.'},
   {q:'d) Which sentence best summarises Karen’s decision?', opts:['Karen rejected the proposal because of Lucy’s concerns.','Karen accepted the proposal of three days at home for everyone.','Karen proposed a compromise: two days at home, new starters in the office full-time for three months, and a review in the spring.','Karen asked Ravi and Lucy to find a solution by the spring.'], ans:2, hint:'Listen to her last turn: how many days, and who is treated differently?', why:'<i>two days at home rather than three, and new starters in the office full-time for their first three months. We’ll review it in the spring.</i>'}],
  sol:'<p><b>Summary for the team.</b> Karen proposed allowing everyone to work from home three days a week. Ravi strongly supported the idea, as it would save him nearly three hours of commuting a day, whereas Lucy was worried that new starters would miss out on learning from experienced colleagues. Ravi admitted that this was a fair point; even so, he suggested a fixed office day for training. In the end, Karen proposed a compromise: two days at home, new starters in the office full-time for their first three months, and a review in the spring.</p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'Peer review: an essay draft',
  p:`<p>A classmate asks you to review the draft of an argumentative essay before handing it in. Read it carefully, then answer the questions one by one.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Draft essay: “Young people should move to the city.” Discuss."><p lang="en">(1) Nowadays, many young people live in cities. Cities are big and there are a lot of things to do there.</p><p lang="en">(2) Despite of the high cost of living, cities offer far more job opportunities. Moreover, most universities are located there, and cultural life is much richer.</p><p lang="en">(3) Cities offer more jobs. On the contrary, rents are much higher, but many young people are willing to pay them.</p><p lang="en">(4) In conclusion, cities are better. Also, the countryside has very bad internet connections.</p></div>`,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) What is the main weakness of paragraph 1?', opts:['It is too long.','It has no clear thesis: the reader doesn’t know what the writer will argue.','It uses too many linkers.','It is written in a formal register.'], ans:1, hint:'Look at the role of an introduction in an argumentative essay.', why:'An introduction should end with a thesis, for example <i>I believe that, for most young people, the advantages of city life outweigh the drawbacks.</i> Here, the paragraph only describes.'},
   {q:'b) Correct the first sentence of paragraph 2 and write it out in full.', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V(['Despite','In spite of'],' the high cost of living, cities offer far more job opportunities.').concat(V(['Although','Even though'],' the cost of living is high, cities offer far more job opportunities.'),['Despite the fact that the cost of living is high, cities offer far more job opportunities.','Cities offer far more job opportunities, despite the high cost of living.']), hint:'One small word is wrong after <i>Despite</i>.', why:'<i>Despite the high cost of living, cities offer far more job opportunities.</i> No <i>of</i> after <i>despite</i>; or use <i>In spite of</i>.'},
   {q:'c) In paragraph 3, <i>On the contrary</i> is wrong. Which linker should replace it? Write it.', lab:'The linker', txt:['On the other hand','However','That said','Even so','Then again','At the same time','By contrast','Nevertheless','Nonetheless'], hint:'The writer isn’t contradicting the first sentence, but presenting the other side.', why:'<i>On the other hand</i> (or <i>However</i>) presents the other side of the question. <i>On the contrary</i> would mean that cities do <b>not</b> offer more jobs.'},
   {q:'d) Rewrite the second sentence of paragraph 3 so that it begins with <i>Although</i>, and remove <i>but</i>: “Rents are much higher, but many young people are willing to pay them.”', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V(['Although','Even though'],' rents are much higher',['',' in cities'],', many young people are willing to pay them.'), hint:'With <i>although</i>, there is no <i>but</i>: one comma separates the two clauses.', why:'<i>Although rents are much higher, many young people are willing to pay them.</i> <i>Although … but</i> is a classic mistake.'},
   {q:'e) What is wrong with the conclusion (paragraph 4)?', opts:['It is too formal.','It is too long and detailed.','It uses <i>In conclusion</i>, which is not allowed in essays.','It is too vague, and it introduces a new argument (the internet) instead of weighing up the ones already given.'], ans:3, hint:'Remember the two rules for a conclusion.', why:'A conclusion restates the thesis in new words and weighs the arguments: <i>On balance, despite higher rents, the opportunities cities offer make them the better choice for most young people.</i> The internet argument belongs in a body paragraph.'}],
  sol:'<p>The draft needs: a clear <b>thesis</b> at the end of the introduction; <i>Despite the high cost of living</i> (no <i>of</i>); <i>On the other hand</i> instead of <i>On the contrary</i>; <i>Although rents are much higher, many young people are willing to pay them</i> (no <i>but</i>); and a conclusion that weighs up the arguments without adding a new one. A possible conclusion: <i>On balance, although city life comes at a cost, the opportunities it offers make it the better choice for most young people.</i></p>'},
]},

/* ===================================================================== */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-words', o:9, h:'Word power', p:'Build and choose words precisely: prefixes and suffixes, whole word families, and the collocations that make English sound natural (<i>make a decision, heavy rain, highly unlikely</i>).', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'Editing a draft',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you’ll be able to build new words from the ones you know with prefixes and suffixes, choose the right form for each gap in a sentence, and use natural collocations instead of word-for-word translations.</p>
<p>Nadia edits a local news website. She gives Josh, a young journalist, feedback on his article about a new library. <b>Listen without reading</b> first, twice if you like.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> What does Nadia think of Josh’s article?</p>`,
  audio:[['Nadia','I’ve read your piece on the new library, Josh. It’s lively, and the interviews are great. But there are a few things to fix before it goes online.'],['Josh','Go on, I can take it.'],['Nadia','First, you say the council “took a decision” in March. That’s not wrong in British English, but “made a decision” sounds more natural. And a bit further down, the opening day was a success “despite the strong rain”.'],['Josh','Heavy rain. Of course. I always mix those up.'],['Nadia','Then there’s “the unrespect of the rules”. The word you want is “disrespect for the rules”.'],['Josh','Ah, I was thinking of the French word, “irrespect”.'],['Nadia','I guessed. And “the project has done a lot of progress” should be “has made a lot of progress”. Apart from that, it’s really readable. Make those changes and it’s good to go.'],['Josh','Thanks, Nadia. I’ll sort it out now.']], cache:true,
  opts:['It needs to be completely rewritten.','The interviews are weak, but the language is perfect.','It’s good, but a few word choices and collocations need correcting.','It’s far too long and should be cut.'], ans:2,
  why:'Nadia praises the article (<i>lively, the interviews are great, really readable</i>) and lists a few fixes: <i>made a decision, heavy rain, disrespect for, made progress</i>. Once they’re done, <i>it’s good to go</i>.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Collocations with make, do, take, have, pay',
  p:`<p>A <b>collocation</b> is a group of words that naturally go together. French speakers often translate « faire » and « prendre » word for word, and the result sounds odd. Learn these as whole chunks. Tap a line to hear it. In the vocabulary review, answer with the whole collocation.</p>`,
  mots:[['to make a decision','prendre une décision','We need to make a decision by Friday.'],['to make a mistake','faire une erreur','Everyone makes mistakes.'],['to make progress','faire des progrès','She’s made a lot of progress this year.'],['to make an effort','faire un effort','He made an effort to be polite.'],['to make a difference','changer les choses, faire la différence','Small changes can make a big difference.'],['to make a complaint','faire une réclamation, se plaindre officiellement','I’d like to make a complaint.'],['to do research','faire des recherches','I did some research before buying.'],['to do (sb) a favour','rendre service (à qn)','Could you do me a favour?'],['to do damage','causer des dégâts','The storm did a lot of damage.'],['to do sport','faire du sport','I try to do some sport every week.'],['to take place','avoir lieu','The meeting will take place on Monday.'],['to take a risk','prendre un risque','Sometimes you have to take risks.'],['to have a look','jeter un coup d’œil','Can I have a look?'],['to pay attention (to)','faire attention (à)','Pay attention to the details.'],['heavy rain','une forte pluie','The match was cancelled because of heavy rain.'],['heavy traffic','une circulation dense','We got stuck in heavy traffic.'],['strong coffee','un café fort, serré','I need a strong coffee.'],['highly unlikely','très peu probable','It’s highly unlikely that he’ll come.'],['fully aware (of)','pleinement conscient (de)','I’m fully aware of the risks.'],['bitterly disappointed','amèrement déçu','The fans were bitterly disappointed.']],
  note:'<b>Pour les francophones :</b> « faire » se partage entre <i>make</i> et <i>do</i>, et parfois un autre verbe : « faire attention » = <i>pay attention</i>, « faire une promenade » = <i>go for a walk</i>, « faire la queue » = <i>queue</i>. Une méthode plus loin t’aide à choisir.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Recall: a decision to make',
  p:`<p>Translate into English, using a collocation from the list.</p><p><b>Question:</b> How do you say « Nous devons prendre une décision avant vendredi. » in English?</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('We ',['need to','have to','must','have got to','should'],' ',['make','take','reach','come to'],' a decision ',['before','by'],' Friday.'),
  why:'<i>We need to make a decision by Friday.</i> <i>Make a decision</i> is the safe, natural choice everywhere. <i>Take a decision</i> also exists in British English, especially in formal or business contexts, but French speakers tend to overuse it.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Prefixes: changing the meaning',
  p:`<p>In Josh’s article, <i>unrespect</i> doesn’t exist: the opposite of <i>respect</i> is <b>disrespect</b>. English builds thousands of words by adding a <b>prefix</b> at the start. A prefix changes the meaning but not the word class: <i>happy</i> and <i>unhappy</i> are both adjectives. The difficulty is choosing the right one.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Negative prefixes</b>:</p><ul><li><b>un-</b>, the most common: <i>unhappy, unlikely, unable, unexpected, unusual, uncomfortable, unavailable</i>.</li><li><b>in-</b>: <i>inaccurate, inefficient, insecure</i>; <b>im-</b> before <i>m</i> or <i>p</i>: <i>impossible, impatient, immature</i>; <b>il-</b> before <i>l</i>: <i>illegal, illogical</i>; <b>ir-</b> before <i>r</i>: <i>irresponsible, irregular, irrelevant</i>.</li><li><b>dis-</b>: <i>dishonest, disagree, disappear, disrespect, dislike</i>. <b>non-</b>: <i>non-existent, non-smoker, non-fiction</i>.</li><li><b>mis-</b> = wrongly: <i>misunderstand, mislead, misspell, misuse</i>.</li></ul><p><b>Other prefixes</b>: <b>over-</b> (too much: <i>overcrowded, overestimate</i>), <b>under-</b> (not enough: <i>underpaid, underestimate</i>), <b>re-</b> (again: <i>rebuild, rethink</i>), <b>pre-</b> and <b>post-</b> (before, after: <i>pre-war, postgraduate</i>), <b>co-</b> (together: <i>co-author</i>), <b>self-</b> (<i>self-confident</i>), <b>ex-</b> (former: <i>ex-boss</i>), <b>anti-</b> (against: <i>antisocial</i>).</p></div>
<div class="idea"><p>There is no reliable rule for choosing between <i>un-</i> and <i>in-</i>, but <i>un-</i> is the safest guess, especially with words ending in <i>-able, -ed</i> and <i>-ing</i>: <i>unaffordable, unexpected, unconvincing</i>. Learn the negative together with the word.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>The figures were <b>inaccurate</b>, so the report was <b>misleading</b>. We had <b>underestimated</b> the costs and had to <b>rethink</b> the whole project.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> le préfixe négatif diffère souvent du français. « inconfortable » = <i>uncomfortable</i>, « inégal » = <i>unequal</i>, « inhabituel » = <i>unusual</i>, « inattendu » = <i>unexpected</i>, « indisponible » = <i>unavailable</i>, « irrespect » = <i>disrespect</i>, « malhonnête » = <i>dishonest</i>. Deux pièges célèbres : <i>inflammable</i> veut dire « qui brûle facilement », comme <i>flammable</i> ; et <i>invaluable</i> veut dire « inestimable, extrêmement précieux », pas « sans valeur » (= <i>worthless</i>).</p></div>`,
  audio:'unlikely. unexpected. inaccurate. impossible. illegal. irresponsible. dishonest. misunderstand. overcrowded. underestimate. The figures were inaccurate, so the report was misleading.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Suffixes: changing the word class',
  p:`<p>Nadia’s corrections also depend on the <b>word class</b>: after <i>the</i> you need a noun, before a noun an adjective, after a verb often an adverb. <b>Suffixes</b> change the class of a word, so a single root can produce a whole family: <i>decide, decision, decisive, indecisive, decisively</i>.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>Nouns</b>: <b>-tion, -sion</b> (<i>explain → explanation, decide → decision</i>), <b>-ment</b> (<i>develop → development, argue → argument</i>), <b>-ness</b> (<i>aware → awareness, kind → kindness</i>), <b>-ity</b> (<i>able → ability, responsible → responsibility</i>), <b>-ance, -ence</b> (<i>perform → performance, depend → dependence</i>), <b>-ship, -hood</b> (<i>friendship, childhood</i>); people: <b>-er, -or, -ee, -ist</b> (<i>employer, employee, scientist</i>).</li><li><b>Adjectives</b>: <b>-ful, -less</b> (<i>useful, useless</i>), <b>-able, -ible</b> (<i>reliable, flexible</i>), <b>-ous</b> (<i>dangerous, ambitious</i>), <b>-ive</b> (<i>creative, decisive</i>), <b>-al</b> (<i>cultural, financial</i>), <b>-ic</b> (<i>economic, scientific</i>).</li><li><b>Verbs</b>: <b>-ise</b> (<i>modernise</i>; <i>-ize</i> in American English), <b>-en</b> (<i>widen, shorten, strengthen</i>), <b>-ify</b> (<i>simplify, clarify</i>), and <b>en-</b> at the start (<i>enable, enlarge</i>).</li><li><b>Adverbs</b>: <b>-ly</b> (<i>careful → carefully, basic → basically, true → truly</i>).</li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>Many words combine a prefix <b>and</b> a suffix: <i>bear → bearable → unbearable → unbearably</i>; <i>employ → employment → unemployment</i>; <i>able → ability → inability</i>. Faced with a new word, take it apart: you can often guess its meaning from the pieces.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>The <b>development</b> of the app took two years. It is <b>reliable</b>, <b>surprisingly</b> fast, and the <b>simplicity</b> of its design is its main <b>strength</b>.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones, l’orthographe :</b> <i>pronounce</i> → <b>pronunciation</b> (pas <i>pronounciation</i>) ; <b>responsibility</b> (avec <i>i</i>, pas <i>responsability</i>) ; <b>development</b> (un seul <i>p</i>, pas de <i>e</i> après le <i>p</i>) ; <b>independent</b> (pas <i>-ant</i>) ; <b>example, address, comfortable</b>. Et <i>argue</i> perd son <i>e</i> : <b>argument</b>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'decide, decision, decisive, indecisive, decisively. explain, explanation. develop, development. responsible, responsibility. pronounce, pronunciation.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Filling a word-formation gap',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> whenever you have a root word and must fit the right form into a sentence, in an exercise, but also when you write and hesitate between <i>succeed, success, successful</i> and <i>successfully</i>.</p><p><b>Why it works:</b> the words around the gap tell you which word class is needed, and the meaning of the sentence tells you whether the word is positive or negative. Only then do you choose the prefix and the suffix.</p><p><b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li><b>Word class</b>: after a determiner (<i>the, a, his</i>) → noun; before a noun or after <i>be, seem</i> → adjective; modifying a verb or an adjective → adverb; after <i>to</i> or a modal → verb.</li><li><b>Meaning</b>: positive or negative? If negative, which prefix?</li><li><b>Number and form</b>: singular or plural noun? Which tense for a verb?</li><li><b>Spelling</b>: dropped <i>e</i>, changed <i>y</i>, doubled consonant.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Complete with the right form of the word in capitals: “The company’s ___ to adapt to online shopping led to its collapse.” (ABLE)',
   etapes:[{f:'Word class: after <i>The company’s</i> and before <i>to adapt</i> → a <b>noun</b>.',w:'A possessive (<i>the company’s</i>) is followed by a noun, just like <i>its</i> or <i>his</i>.'},
    {f:'From <i>able</i>, the noun is <i>ability</i>.',w:'Suffix <i>-ity</i>, with a spelling change: <i>able → ability</i>.'},
    {f:'Meaning: the company <b>collapsed</b>, so it could <b>not</b> adapt → negative: <i>inability</i>.',w:'The opposite of <i>ability</i> takes <i>in-</i>, not <i>un-</i> (although the adjective is <i>unable</i>).'},
    {f:'Number: one company, one inability → singular.',w:'Always reread the whole sentence with your word in it.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>The company’s <b>inability</b> to adapt to online shopping led to its collapse.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: build the noun',
  p:`<p>Complete the sentence with the right form of the word in capitals.</p><p>“Staff turnover has fallen since conditions improved. Employee ___ is now at its highest level for ten years.” (SATISFY)</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the missing word.</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['satisfaction'],
  why:'After <i>Employee</i> (used like an adjective) and before <i>is</i>, a noun is needed: <i>satisfy → satisfaction</i>. The sentence is positive, so no prefix.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a negative adjective',
  p:`<p>Complete the sentence with the right form of the word in capitals.</p><p>“The figures in the report were completely ___, so we had to check every one of them again.” (ACCURATE)</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the missing word.</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['inaccurate'],
  why:'After <i>were completely</i>, an adjective; the figures had to be checked, so the meaning is negative: <i>inaccurate</i> (prefix <i>in-</i>).'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a double change',
  p:`<p>Complete the sentence with the right form of the word in capitals. You will need a prefix <b>and</b> suffixes.</p><p>“The film was ___ long: I nearly fell asleep twice.” (BEAR)</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the missing word.</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['unbearably'],
  why:'The gap modifies the adjective <i>long</i>, so you need an adverb; the meaning is negative. <i>bear → bearable → unbearable → unbearably</i>.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Make or do?',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> every time French says « faire » in front of a noun, and often « prendre ».</p><p><b>Why it works:</b> English splits « faire » between two verbs with different cores. <b>make</b> = create, produce, cause a result: something exists afterwards that didn’t exist before. <b>do</b> = perform an activity, a task or a job, often without a visible product.</p><p><b>How</b>, in three questions:</p>
<ol><li><b>Is something created, produced or caused?</b> → <i>make</i>: <i>make a cake, a plan, a noise, a mess, money, a decision, a mistake, progress, a complaint</i>.</li><li><b>Is it work, a task, an activity, or something vague</b> (<i>something, anything, nothing</i>)? → <i>do</i>: <i>do the shopping, homework, research, a course, business, the washing-up, exercise, sport, your best, something</i>.</li><li><b>Is it a fixed expression?</b> Learn these: <i>make an effort, make friends, make a phone call, make an excuse, make sure</i>; <i>do somebody a favour, do damage, do harm, do good</i>.</li></ol>
<p>Then put the verb in the right tense: <i>made, did, done</i>.</p>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Complete: “I ___ a lot of mistakes in my first year, so I ___ an evening course, and now I ___ much better work.”',
   etapes:[{f:'<i>___ a lot of mistakes</i> → <b>made</b>.',w:'A mistake is a result you produce: <i>make a mistake</i>. Past tense: <i>made</i>.'},
    {f:'<i>___ an evening course</i> → <b>did</b>.',w:'A course is an activity you follow: <i>do a course</i>. Past tense: <i>did</i>.'},
    {f:'<i>now I ___ much better work</i> → <b>do</b>.',w:'<i>work</i> is the typical <i>do</i> noun: <i>do work, do a job</i>. Present: <i>do</i>.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>I <b>made</b> a lot of mistakes in my first year, so I <b>did</b> an evening course, and now I <b>do</b> much better work.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Collocations beyond make and do',
  p:`<p><i>Strong rain</i> is grammatically correct and perfectly understandable, yet native speakers never say it: the collocation is <b>heavy rain</b>. Collocations can’t be worked out by logic. They are habits of the language, and using them is what makes your English sound natural rather than translated.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>Verb + noun</b>: <i>take</i> (place, part in, a risk, a photo, notes, advantage of, responsibility for); <i>have</i> (a look, a break, a chat, a go, a shower, breakfast); <i>pay</i> (attention, a compliment, a visit); <i>keep / break</i> (a promise, a secret); <i>raise</i> (a question, awareness, money); <i>give</i> (advice, a speech, a presentation).</li><li><b>Adjective + noun</b>: <i>heavy</i> (rain, traffic, smoker, workload), <i>strong</i> (coffee, wind, accent, opinion), <i>high</i> (price, temperature, level), <i>deep</i> (sleep, concern), <i>close</i> (friend), <i>narrow</i> (escape).</li><li><b>Adverb + adjective</b>: <i>highly</i> (unlikely, successful, recommended), <i>deeply</i> (concerned, moving), <i>fully</i> (aware, booked), <i>bitterly</i> (cold, disappointed), <i>utterly</i> (ridiculous), <i>seriously</i> (ill), <i>perfectly</i> (clear).</li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>When you learn a noun, learn the verbs and adjectives that go with it. A good learner’s dictionary lists them, and reading in English shows you which combinations come up again and again.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>The conference will <b>take place</b> in June. Rooms are already <b>fully booked</b>, so it’s <b>highly unlikely</b> you’ll find one now. <b>Pay attention</b> to the deadline, and <b>have a look</b> at the programme.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> « prendre une douche, prendre le petit-déjeuner » = <i>have a shower, have breakfast</i> ; « poser une question » = <i>ask a question</i> (pas <i>make a question</i>) ; « gagner de l’argent » = <i>make money</i> ou <i>earn money</i> (<i>win</i> = gagner au jeu) ; « un grand fumeur » = <i>a heavy smoker</i> ; « gravement malade » = <i>seriously ill</i> ; « une forte pluie » = <i>heavy rain</i>, mais « un vent fort » = <i>a strong wind</i>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'The conference will take place in June. Rooms are already fully booked, so it’s highly unlikely you’ll find one now. Pay attention to the deadline, and have a look at the programme.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: make or do',
  p:`<p>Complete with the correct form of <i>make</i> or <i>do</i>.</p><p>“The storm ___ serious damage to the roof last night.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the missing word.</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['did'],
  why:'<i>do damage</i> is a fixed collocation (also <i>do harm, do good</i>), and <i>last night</i> requires the past: <i>did</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: fix the collocations',
  p:`<p>This sentence contains two collocation mistakes typical of French speakers.</p><p>“Last year, our team did a lot of progress, although we also did some expensive mistakes.”</p><p><b>Question:</b> Write the corrected sentence.</p>`,
  lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('Last year, our team made ',['a lot of','lots of','great','a great deal of','real','good'],' progress, although we also made some expensive mistakes.'),
  why:'<i>make progress</i> and <i>make a mistake</i>: both produce a result. Note that <i>progress</i> is uncountable: <i>a lot of progress</i>, never <i>a progress</i> or <i>progresses</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a natural sentence',
  p:`<p>You are writing to a colleague about a meeting planned for tomorrow.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Put the words in order to form a natural sentence.</p>`,
  ordre:['It','is','highly','unlikely','that','the','meeting','will','take','place','tomorrow.'],
  alt:['It is highly unlikely that the meeting will take place tomorrow.','Tomorrow it is highly unlikely that the meeting will take place.'],
  why:'Two collocations: <i>highly unlikely</i> (not <i>strongly unlikely</i>) and <i>take place</i> (« avoir lieu »).'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Stress shift in word families',
  p:`<p>When a suffix is added, the stress often <b>moves</b>. If you keep the stress of the root, listeners may not recognise the word.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>PHO</b>tograph → pho<b>TO</b>grapher → photo<b>GRAPH</b>ic</li><li>e<b>CON</b>omy → eco<b>NOM</b>ic</li><li><b>POL</b>itics → po<b>LIT</b>ical → poli<b>TI</b>cian</li><li><b>A</b>ble → a<b>BIL</b>ity; <b>AN</b>alyse → a<b>NAL</b>ysis</li></ul><p>A useful rule: words ending in <b>-ic, -ical, -ity, -tion, -sion, -ian</b> are stressed on the syllable <b>just before</b> the suffix: eco<b>NOM</b>ic, po<b>LIT</b>ical, a<b>BIL</b>ity, edu<b>CA</b>tion, de<b>CI</b>sion, poli<b>TI</b>cian.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Pour les francophones :</b> on a tendance à accentuer la dernière syllabe (« photograPHER ») et à prononcer toutes les voyelles pleinement. Dans <i>photographer</i>, le premier <i>o</i> est faible : /fəˈtɒɡrəfə/. Dans <i>economic</i>, c’est <i>nom</i> qui est fort : /ˌiːkəˈnɒmɪk/ ou /ˌekəˈnɒmɪk/.</p></div>`,
  audio:'photograph, photographer, photographic. economy, economic. politics, political, politician. able, ability. analyse, analysis.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Repeat: moving stress',
  p:`<p>Listen, then say the sentence. Watch the stress in each word family: poli<b>TI</b>cians, responsi<b>BIL</b>ity, eco<b>NOM</b>ic, de<b>CI</b>sions.</p>`,
  dire:'Politicians rarely take responsibility for economic decisions.',
  note:'Say the long words slowly first, syllable by syllable, then at normal speed. The unstressed syllables should be short and light.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Decision fatigue',
  p:`<p>Read this magazine article. You can also listen to it. Notice the collocations and the word families built on <i>decide</i>.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Mind &amp; Work"><p lang="en"><b>Why good choices get harder as the day goes on</b></p><p lang="en">Have you ever noticed that, after a long day of meetings, you can barely decide what to have for dinner? Psychologists have a name for it: decision fatigue. The idea is that every choice we make, however small, draws on a limited supply of mental energy. By the evening, that supply is running low, and our ability to weigh up options declines.</p><p lang="en">The symptoms are surprisingly predictable. Exhausted decision-makers tend either to avoid choosing altogether, sticking with whatever is already in place, or to act impulsively, grabbing the easiest option without considering the consequences. Shoppers who have spent hours comparing products are more likely to give in to unhealthy snacks at the checkout, and some research suggests that tired professionals, from judges to doctors, make more cautious, routine decisions late in the day.</p><p lang="en">Admittedly, the theory is not without its critics. Some researchers have failed to reproduce key experiments, and others argue that our beliefs about willpower matter as much as any real exhaustion. Even so, the practical advice that follows from it is hard to disagree with.</p><p lang="en">First, make important decisions early, when your mind is fresh. Second, reduce the number of trivial choices: some highly successful people famously wear the same type of clothes every day, so that they have one less thing to think about. Finally, take regular breaks and eat properly, because hunger makes us noticeably less patient and less rational.</p><p lang="en">None of this means that we are powerless. It simply means that good decision-making is not only a question of intelligence, but also of timing.</p></div>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the main idea of the article?</p>`,
  audio:'Why good choices get harder as the day goes on. Have you ever noticed that, after a long day of meetings, you can barely decide what to have for dinner? Psychologists have a name for it: decision fatigue. The idea is that every choice we make, however small, draws on a limited supply of mental energy. By the evening, that supply is running low, and our ability to weigh up options declines. The symptoms are surprisingly predictable. Exhausted decision-makers tend either to avoid choosing altogether, sticking with whatever is already in place, or to act impulsively, grabbing the easiest option without considering the consequences. Shoppers who have spent hours comparing products are more likely to give in to unhealthy snacks at the checkout, and some research suggests that tired professionals, from judges to doctors, make more cautious, routine decisions late in the day. Admittedly, the theory is not without its critics. Some researchers have failed to reproduce key experiments, and others argue that our beliefs about willpower matter as much as any real exhaustion. Even so, the practical advice that follows from it is hard to disagree with. First, make important decisions early, when your mind is fresh. Second, reduce the number of trivial choices: some highly successful people famously wear the same type of clothes every day, so that they have one less thing to think about. Finally, take regular breaks and eat properly, because hunger makes us noticeably less patient and less rational. None of this means that we are powerless. It simply means that good decision-making is not only a question of intelligence, but also of timing.',
  opts:['We make better decisions in the evening, because we have had time to think.','Making many choices can wear down our ability to decide well, so timing and routines matter.','The theory of decision fatigue has been completely disproved by recent research.','Only judges and doctors are affected by decision fatigue.'], ans:1,
  why:'Paragraph 1 states the idea (<i>every choice draws on a limited supply of mental energy</i>), paragraph 4 gives the advice (decide early, reduce trivial choices, take breaks), and the conclusion sums it up: <i>not only a question of intelligence, but also of timing</i>. The critics are mentioned, but the theory isn’t <i>disproved</i>.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Word hunt',
  p:`<p>Look again at paragraph 2 of the article about decision fatigue.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Find the adverb built from the noun <i>impulse</i> (with two suffixes) and write it.</p>`,
  lab:'The word', txt:['impulsively'],
  why:'<i>impulse</i> (noun) → <i>impulsive</i> (adjective, suffix <i>-ive</i>) → <i>impulsively</i> (adverb, suffix <i>-ly</i>): <i>or to act impulsively</i>. It modifies the verb <i>act</i>, so it must be an adverb.'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: collocations',
  p:'Listen and write the sentence exactly. You can listen as many times as you like, and use « Plus lentement ».',
  dictee:'Despite the heavy traffic, she made a real effort to arrive on time.',
  why:'Two collocations: <i>heavy traffic</i> and <i>make an effort</i>, here <i>made a real effort</i>.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'A decision that made a difference',
  p:`<p>Speak for about a minute about a decision that made a difference to your life: a move, a change of studies or job, a new habit. Use at least <b>three collocations</b> with <i>make, do</i> or <i>take</i>, and one adverb + adjective (<i>highly unlikely, fully aware…</i>). Tap « Parler », then « J’ai fini ».</p>`,
  oral:{modele:'Three years ago, I made a decision that really made a difference to my life: I gave up my car and started cycling to work. At first, my friends thought I was taking a big risk, because the traffic in my town is quite heavy. So I did some research, found a safer route along the river, and made an effort to leave home a bit earlier. Honestly, it’s one of the best choices I’ve ever made. I do more exercise, I save money, and I’m fully aware that I’m doing less damage to the environment. The only drawback is the rain: on days of really heavy rain, I’m highly unlikely to arrive at work looking smart!',
   criteres:['I used at least three collocations with make, do or take','I used at least one adjective + noun collocation (heavy traffic, strong coffee…)','I used an adverb + adjective collocation (highly unlikely, fully aware…)','I explained why the decision made a difference','I stressed long words correctly (decision, economic, responsibility…)']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write a review with precise words',
  p:`<p>Write an online review (<b>about 180 words</b>) of a course, a training programme, an app or a place you know well. Give a balanced opinion and a clear recommendation. Use at least <b>five collocations</b> from this unit and at least <b>four words built with a prefix or a suffix</b> (<i>unreliable, overcrowded, enjoyable, clarity…</i>).</p>`,
  ecrire:{min:150, modele:'I signed up for this evening course in digital photography last autumn, and it has made a real difference to the way I take pictures.<br><br>The teacher, a professional photographer, is highly experienced and incredibly patient. She explains even the most technical points with remarkable clarity, and she pays attention to every student’s progress. In twelve weeks, I made more progress than in five years of trying to learn on my own. The final exhibition of our best work was a lovely way to end the course.<br><br>The course is not perfect, though. The classroom was often overcrowded, and the equipment we were given was rather unreliable: twice, a camera stopped working halfway through a session, which was deeply frustrating. Some of the online material was also outdated.<br><br>Nevertheless, I would strongly recommend this course to anyone who wants to move beyond automatic mode. It is affordable, well organised and genuinely enjoyable. Make sure you book early, because places fill up quickly, and do some practice between sessions: it really pays off.<br><br>Overall rating: four stars out of five.',
   criteres:[{t:'I used a collocation with make (a decision, progress, an effort, a difference…)', re:'\\b(make|makes|made|making)\\s+(a\\s+|an\\s+|some\\s+|more\\s+|real\\s+|great\\s+|no\\s+|a real\\s+|a big\\s+|a huge\\s+)?(decision|mistake|progress|effort|difference|complaint|money|sure|friends)'},{t:'I used a collocation with do, take or pay', re:'\\b(do|does|did|doing|done)\\s+(some\\s+|a lot of\\s+|more\\s+)?(research|damage|harm|exercise|sport|homework|practice|business|a course|your best|my best)\\b|\\b(take|takes|took|taking|taken)\\s+(place|part|a risk|risks|pictures|photos|notes)\\b|\\b(pay|pays|paid|paying)\\s+attention\\b'},{t:'I used an adverb + adjective collocation (highly, deeply, fully, bitterly, utterly…)', re:'\\b(highly|deeply|fully|bitterly|utterly|seriously|perfectly|strongly|incredibly|genuinely|remarkably)\\s+\\w+'},{t:'I used words with a prefix (un-, in-, dis-, mis-, over-, under-…)', re:'\\b(un|in|im|il|ir|dis|mis|over|under|out)(reliable|comfortable|crowded|helpful|organised|organized|pleasant|accurate|patient|possible|responsible|regular|appointing|appointed|satisfied|satisfactory|expected|usual|affordable|available|friendly|bearable|necessary|priced|rated|estimated|whelming|whelmed|efficient|convenient|leading|honest|dated|interesting)\\b'},'I used at least four words built with a suffix (-ful, -able, -ness, -ity, -ment, -ly…)','I gave a balanced opinion and a clear recommendation']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'Summary',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li><b>Prefixes</b> change the meaning, not the class: <i>un-</i> (the safest guess), <i>in-/im-/il-/ir-</i>, <i>dis-</i>, <i>non-</i>, <i>mis-</i> (wrongly), <i>over-/under-</i>, <i>re-</i>, <i>co-</i>, <i>self-</i>. Traps: <i>uncomfortable, unusual, disrespect</i>; <i>invaluable</i> = extremely valuable; <i>inflammable</i> = flammable.</li><li><b>Suffixes</b> change the class: nouns <i>-tion, -ment, -ness, -ity, -ance/-ence, -ship</i>; adjectives <i>-ful, -less, -able/-ible, -ous, -ive, -al, -ic</i>; verbs <i>-ise, -en, -ify</i>; adverbs <i>-ly</i>.</li><li><b>Gap method</b>: word class from the words around the gap → positive or negative → number/tense → spelling.</li><li><b>make</b> = create, produce, cause (<i>a decision, a mistake, progress, an effort, money</i>); <b>do</b> = activity, task, work (<i>research, homework, sport, a course, damage, a favour</i>).</li><li><b>Collocations</b>: <i>take place, take a risk, have a look, pay attention, ask a question; heavy rain/traffic/smoker, strong coffee/wind; highly unlikely, fully aware, bitterly disappointed, seriously ill</i>.</li><li>Spelling: <i>pronunciation, responsibility, development, independent, argument</i>.</li><li>Stress moves with suffixes: <b>PHO</b>tograph, pho<b>TO</b>grapher, photo<b>GRAPH</b>ic; before <i>-ic, -ity, -tion, -ian</i>.</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'Proofreading a cover letter',
  p:`<p>A friend who is applying for a job asks you to check her cover letter. Some numbered expressions are wrong; for the words in capitals, she wasn’t sure of the form. Read it, then answer the questions one by one.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Cover letter"><p lang="en">Dear Ms Grant,</p><p lang="en">I am writing to apply for the position of Events Coordinator advertised on your website.</p><p lang="en">In my current role at a busy conference centre, I <b>(1) do all the decisions</b> about suppliers and budgets. Last year, I <b>(2) did a big effort</b> to reduce costs, and we saved almost 15%. I am <b>(3) full aware</b> that your events are larger and that the role involves a <b>(4) heavy workload</b>, but I find this kind of challenge motivating.</p><p lang="en">My colleagues describe me as <b>(5) ______ (RELY)</b> and well organised, and my manager has praised my <b>(6) ______ (ABLE)</b> to stay calm under pressure.</p><p lang="en">I would welcome the opportunity to discuss my application.</p><p lang="en">Yours sincerely,<br>Léa Morel</p></div>`,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) Which of the numbered expressions (1) to (4) is already correct?', opts:['(1) do all the decisions','(2) did a big effort','(4) heavy workload','(3) full aware'], ans:2, hint:'Think of <i>heavy rain, heavy traffic</i>…', why:'<i>a heavy workload</i> is a natural collocation (« une lourde charge de travail »). The other three need correcting.'},
   {q:'b) Correct expression (1). Write the corrected words.', lab:'Correction', txt:['make all the decisions','take all the decisions','make all decisions','I make all the decisions','I take all the decisions'], hint:'A decision is something you produce.', why:'<i>make all the decisions</i> (<i>take</i> is possible in British business English). Never <i>do a decision</i>.'},
   {q:'c) Correct expression (2). Write the corrected words.', lab:'Correction', txt:['made a big effort','made a great effort','made a huge effort','made a real effort','made a special effort','I made a big effort'], hint:'Same verb as for <i>progress</i> and <i>mistakes</i>.', why:'<i>make an effort</i>: <i>I made a big effort to reduce costs.</i> Expression (3) also needs an adverb: <i>fully aware</i>.'},
   {q:'d) Write the right form of RELY for gap (5).', lab:'The word', txt:['reliable'], hint:'After <i>describe me as</i>, an adjective; the meaning is positive.', why:'<i>rely → reliable</i> (the <i>y</i> becomes <i>i</i> before <i>-able</i>): « fiable ».'},
   {q:'e) Write the right form of ABLE for gap (6).', lab:'The word', txt:['ability'], hint:'After <i>my</i>, a noun. Is the meaning positive?', why:'After the possessive <i>my</i>, a noun: <i>ability</i>. The meaning is positive (praise), so no prefix: not <i>inability</i>.'}],
  sol:'<p>Corrected letter: <i>I <b>make all the decisions</b> about suppliers and budgets. Last year, I <b>made a big effort</b> to reduce costs… I am <b>fully aware</b> that your events are larger and that the role involves a heavy workload… My colleagues describe me as <b>reliable</b> and well organised, and my manager has praised my <b>ability</b> to stay calm under pressure.</i> <i>Heavy workload</i> was already correct.</p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A podcast on new words',
  p:'You are listening to a short podcast episode about how English creates new words. Listen carefully, as many times as you need, then answer the questions one by one. Some questions ask you to build words yourself.',
  audio:'Every year, dictionaries add hundreds of new words, and most of them aren’t really new at all. They’re built from old pieces. Take “unfriend”. The prefix “un” has been around for more than a thousand years, but sticking it onto “friend” to mean removing someone from your contacts online is a twenty-first-century idea. Many people found it unacceptable at first; now hardly anyone notices it. Suffixes are just as productive. Add “able” to almost any verb and you get an adjective: a series you can watch in one go is “bingeable”. Then there are blends, where two words are squeezed together: “brunch” is breakfast plus lunch, and a “staycation” is a holiday you spend at home. What’s remarkable is how quickly we understand these words the first time we hear them. That’s because we already know the rules, even if nobody ever taught them to us.', cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) What is the speaker’s main point?', opts:['Most new words are borrowed from other languages.','Dictionaries refuse to accept new words.','Most new words are built from existing pieces, following rules that speakers already know.','Blends are the only way new words are created.'], ans:2, hint:'Listen to the second and last sentences.', why:'<i>Most of them aren’t really new at all. They’re built from old pieces</i>, and <i>we already know the rules</i>.'},
   {q:'b) According to the speaker, how did many people react to “unfriend” at first? Write the adjective built from ACCEPT.', lab:'The word', txt:['unacceptable'], hint:'A negative prefix and a suffix.', why:'<i>Many people found it unacceptable at first</i>: <i>accept → acceptable → unacceptable</i>.'},
   {q:'c) What is a blend, according to the podcast?', opts:['A word with both a prefix and a suffix','Two words squeezed together into one, like <i>brunch</i>','A word borrowed from French','A word that changes its stress'], ans:1, hint:'Listen to the examples <i>brunch</i> and <i>staycation</i>.', why:'<i>brunch</i> = <i>breakfast</i> + <i>lunch</i>; <i>staycation</i> = <i>stay</i> + <i>vacation</i>.'},
   {q:'d) Complete with the adverb built from the adjective <i>remarkable</i>: “We understand these words ___ quickly.”', lab:'The word', txt:['remarkably'], hint:'It modifies the adverb <i>quickly</i>.', why:'<i>remarkable → remarkably</i>: <i>-able</i> becomes <i>-ably</i>.'},
   {q:'e) Using a prefix from this unit, write the verb that means « mal comprendre ».', lab:'The verb', txt:['misunderstand','to misunderstand'], hint:'The prefix that means “wrongly”.', why:'<i>mis-</i> = wrongly: <i>misunderstand</i>, like <i>mislead, misspell, misuse</i>.'}],
  sol:'<p>The speaker explains that most new words are built from existing parts: prefixes (<i>un- + friend</i>), suffixes (<i>binge + -able</i>) and blends (<i>breakfast + lunch = brunch</i>). Many people found <i>unfriend</i> <b>unacceptable</b> at first. We understand such words <b>remarkably</b> quickly because we already know the rules. With the prefix <i>mis-</i>: <b>misunderstand</b>.</p>'},
]},

);
})();
